WEBVTT

02:30.480 --> 02:32.153
<i>MAN ON RADIO:
Men, are you over 40?</i>

02:32.240 --> 02:34.960
<i>When you wake up in the morning,
do you feel tired and rundown?</i>

02:35.040 --> 02:36.216
<i>Do you have that
listless feeling?</i>

02:36.240 --> 02:37.240
(CHANGES STATION)

02:37.280 --> 02:38.430
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

02:38.520 --> 02:40.079
(ALARM RINGING)

03:13.440 --> 03:15.272
(CHILDREN CHATTERING)

04:08.880 --> 04:10.439
(PHONE RINGING)

04:15.320 --> 04:16.356
Jefferies.

04:16.440 --> 04:17.616
<i>GUNNISON:
Congratulations, Jeff.</i>

04:17.640 --> 04:18.640
For what?

04:18.680 --> 04:20.399
<i>For getting rid of that cast.</i>

04:20.480 --> 04:22.437
Who said I was
getting rid of it?

04:22.520 --> 04:23.954
<i>This is Wednesday.</i>

04:24.040 --> 04:27.829
<i>Seven weeks from the day
you broke your leg. Yes or no?</i>

04:28.920 --> 04:33.551
Gunnison, how did you ever get to be such
a big editor with such a small memory?

04:33.640 --> 04:35.597
<i>Thrift, industry
and hard work.</i>

04:35.680 --> 04:38.752
<i>And catching the publisher
with his secretary.</i>

04:38.880 --> 04:39.880
<i>Did I get the wrong day?</i>

04:39.920 --> 04:41.593
No.

04:41.680 --> 04:43.433
No, wrong week.

04:43.520 --> 04:48.151
Next Wednesday I emerge
from this plaster cocoon.

04:48.240 --> 04:50.277
<i>That's too bad, Jeff.</i>

04:50.360 --> 04:53.159
<i>Well, I guess I can't
be lucky every day.</i>

04:53.240 --> 04:54.594
<i>Forget I called.</i>

04:54.680 --> 04:57.957
Yeah, I sure feel sorry
for you, Gunnison.

04:58.040 --> 05:01.670
Must be rough on you thinking of me
wearing this cast for another whole week.

05:01.760 --> 05:04.639
<i>That one week is gonna cost me
my best photographer</i>

05:04.720 --> 05:06.951
<i>and you a big assignment.</i>

05:07.040 --> 05:08.040
Where?

05:08.160 --> 05:09.833
<i>There's no point in
even talking about it.</i>

05:09.920 --> 05:11.513
Oh, come on, come on. Where?

05:11.600 --> 05:13.159
<i>Kashmir.</i>

05:13.240 --> 05:15.835
<i>Got a code tip
from the bureau chief this morning.</i>

05:15.960 --> 05:17.679
<i>The place is about
to go up in smoke.</i>

05:17.760 --> 05:20.521
What did I tell you? Didn't I tell you
that's the next place to watch?

05:20.600 --> 05:21.600
<i>You did.</i>

05:21.680 --> 05:24.036
Okay. When do I leave?
Half-hour? Hour?

05:24.120 --> 05:26.316
<i>With that cast on? You don't.</i>

05:26.400 --> 05:28.676
Stop sounding stuffy.

05:28.760 --> 05:32.640
I can take pictures from a jeep
or a water buffalo, if necessary.

05:32.720 --> 05:35.838
<i>You're too valuable to the magazine
for us to play around with.</i>

05:35.920 --> 05:37.752
<i>I'll send Morgan or Lambert.</i>

05:37.840 --> 05:39.752
Morgan or Lambert.
That's fine.

05:40.640 --> 05:42.950
I get myself
half-killed for you,

05:43.040 --> 05:45.600
and you reward me
by stealing my assignments.

05:45.680 --> 05:49.356
<i>I didn't ask you to stand in the middle
of that automobile racetrack.</i>

05:49.440 --> 05:53.957
You asked for something
dramatically different. You got it.

05:54.080 --> 05:56.470
<i>So did you. Goodbye, Jeff.</i>

05:56.560 --> 06:00.031
Now wait a minute, Gunnison.
You've got to get me out of here.

06:00.120 --> 06:02.555
Six weeks sitting in
a two-room apartment

06:02.640 --> 06:04.936
with nothing to do but look out
the window at the neighbors.

06:04.960 --> 06:05.960
<i>Bye, Jeff.</i>

06:06.040 --> 06:07.360
No, Gunnison, I...

06:07.440 --> 06:10.239
If you don't pull me out
of this swamp of boredom,

06:10.320 --> 06:12.152
I'm gonna do
something drastic.

06:12.240 --> 06:13.240
<i>Like what?</i>

06:13.280 --> 06:14.416
"Like what?"
I'm gonna get married.

06:14.440 --> 06:16.716
And then I'll never
be able to go anywhere.

06:16.800 --> 06:18.154
<i>It's about time
you got married,</i>

06:18.240 --> 06:21.551
<i>before you turn into a lonesome
and bitter old man.</i>

06:22.800 --> 06:25.031
Yeah, can't you just see me?

06:26.400 --> 06:28.073
Rushing home to
a hot apartment

06:28.200 --> 06:32.160
to listen to the automatic laundry
and the electric dishwasher

06:32.240 --> 06:36.917
and the garbage disposal
and the nagging wife.

06:37.040 --> 06:40.556
<i>Jeff, wives don't nag
anymore, they discuss.</i>

06:40.640 --> 06:43.235
Is that so? Is that so?

06:43.320 --> 06:45.915
Well, maybe in the high-rent
district they discuss.

06:46.000 --> 06:48.151
In my neighborhood,
they still nag.

06:48.240 --> 06:52.678
<i>Yeah? Well, you know best.
I'll call you later, Jeff.</i>

06:52.760 --> 06:55.753
Yeah, have some good news
the next time, huh?

07:49.480 --> 07:51.836
Good morning.
I said, "Good morning!"

07:51.920 --> 07:53.718
Oh, good morning.

08:11.720 --> 08:14.519
Say, I wouldn't dig
so deep if I were you.

08:15.720 --> 08:18.315
You're giving them
far too much water.

08:19.720 --> 08:21.598
Why don't you shut up?

08:24.880 --> 08:26.075
Well!

08:28.440 --> 08:29.440
I do declare.

08:29.520 --> 08:33.355
New York State sentence for a Peeping Tom is
six months in the workhouse.

08:33.440 --> 08:34.954
Oh, hello, Stella.

08:35.040 --> 08:37.680
And they got no windows
in the workhouse.

08:37.760 --> 08:41.754
You know, in the old days, they used
to put your eyes out with a red-hot poker.

08:41.840 --> 08:46.756
Any of those bikini bombshells
you're always watching worth a red-hot poker?

08:46.880 --> 08:50.920
Oh, dear. We've become
a race of Peeping Toms.

08:51.640 --> 08:55.873
What people ought to do is get outside
their own house and look in for a change.

08:55.960 --> 08:57.280
Yes, sir.

08:57.360 --> 08:59.477
How's that for a bit
of homespun philosophy?

08:59.600 --> 09:01.990
<i>Reader's Digest,</i>
April, 1939.

09:02.120 --> 09:04.237
- Well, I only quote from the best.
- Yeah.

09:04.320 --> 09:06.551
You don't have to take my
temperature this morning.

09:06.640 --> 09:09.394
Quiet. See if you
can break 100.

09:09.480 --> 09:11.870
You know, I should have
been a gypsy fortune-teller

09:11.960 --> 09:14.031
instead of
an insurance-company nurse.

09:14.120 --> 09:18.433
I got a nose for trouble.
Can smell it ten miles away.

09:18.520 --> 09:22.878
You heard of that market crash in '29?
I predicted that.

09:22.960 --> 09:25.111
(MUMBLING)
Just how did you do that, Stella?

09:25.200 --> 09:26.475
Oh, simple.

09:26.560 --> 09:30.076
I was nursing
a director of General Motors.

09:30.160 --> 09:34.279
"Kidney ailment," they said.
"Nerves," I said.

09:34.360 --> 09:38.513
Then I asked myself, "What's General Motors
got to be nervous about?"

09:39.480 --> 09:42.791
"Overproduction," I says.
"Collapse."

09:42.880 --> 09:46.476
When General Motors has to
go to the bathroom 10 times a day,

09:46.560 --> 09:49.075
the whole country's
ready to let go.

09:50.800 --> 09:52.439
You know, Stella,
in economics,

09:52.520 --> 09:55.240
a kidney ailment has no relationship
to the stock market.

09:55.320 --> 09:56.470
None whatsoever.

09:56.560 --> 09:58.597
Crashed, didn't it?

09:58.680 --> 10:01.718
I can smell trouble right here
in this apartment.

10:01.800 --> 10:06.397
First you smash your leg, then you
get to looking out the window,

10:06.480 --> 10:09.154
see things you shouldn't see.
Trouble.

10:10.600 --> 10:12.398
I can see you in court now,

10:12.480 --> 10:15.951
surrounded by a bunch of lawyers
in double-breasted suits.

10:16.040 --> 10:21.240
You're pleading. You say, "Judge, it was only
a little bit of innocent fun.

10:21.320 --> 10:24.313
"I love my neighbors,
like a father."

10:24.400 --> 10:26.437
And the judge says,
"Well, congratulations.

10:26.560 --> 10:29.871
"You've just given birth
to three years in Dannemora."

10:29.960 --> 10:32.634
Right now I'd welcome
trouble, you know.

10:32.720 --> 10:34.234
You've got a
hormone deficiency.

10:34.360 --> 10:36.033
How can you tell
from a thermometer?

10:36.120 --> 10:38.032
Those bathing beauties
you've been watching

10:38.120 --> 10:41.591
haven't raised your temperature
one degree in a month.

10:44.440 --> 10:45.794
Here we go.

10:51.640 --> 10:53.359
One more week.

10:57.240 --> 11:00.233
No, I think you're right. I think
there is gonna be trouble around here.

11:00.320 --> 11:01.754
I knew it.

11:02.080 --> 11:03.116
Ooh!

11:03.200 --> 11:05.396
Do you ever heat that stuff?

11:05.480 --> 11:07.233
Gives your circulation
something to fight.

11:07.320 --> 11:08.320
Oh, I see.

11:08.400 --> 11:09.959
What kind of trouble?

11:10.040 --> 11:11.793
Lisa Fremont.

11:11.880 --> 11:13.075
You kidding?

11:13.160 --> 11:17.837
She's a beautiful young girl, and you're
a reasonably healthy young man.

11:17.920 --> 11:19.240
She expects me to marry her.

11:19.320 --> 11:20.356
That's normal.

11:20.440 --> 11:21.635
I don't want to.

11:21.720 --> 11:22.720
That's abnormal.

11:22.800 --> 11:25.440
I just... I'm not
ready for marriage.

11:25.520 --> 11:28.672
Every man's ready for marriage
when the right girl comes along.

11:28.760 --> 11:31.673
And Lisa Fremont is the right girl
for any man with half a brain

11:31.760 --> 11:33.194
who can get one eye open.

11:33.280 --> 11:34.873
She's all right.

11:34.960 --> 11:36.872
- What'd you do, have a fight?
- No.

11:36.960 --> 11:38.519
Father loading
up the shotgun?

11:38.600 --> 11:39.636
What?

11:39.760 --> 11:40.910
Please, Stella.

11:41.000 --> 11:42.514
It's happened
before, you know.

11:42.600 --> 11:44.159
Some of the world's
happiest marriages

11:44.240 --> 11:47.756
have started "under the gun,"
as you might say.

11:47.840 --> 11:49.200
No, she's just not
the girl for me.

11:49.240 --> 11:50.390
Yeah, she's only perfect.

11:50.520 --> 11:55.515
She's too perfect. She's too talented.
She's too beautiful. She's too sophisticated.

11:55.600 --> 11:57.637
She's too everything
but what I want.

11:57.720 --> 12:01.077
Is what you want something
you can discuss?

12:01.160 --> 12:03.595
What? It's very
simple, Stella.

12:03.680 --> 12:07.754
She belongs to that rarefied atmosphere
of Park Avenue, you know.

12:07.840 --> 12:11.038
Expensive restaurants and
literary cocktail parties.

12:11.120 --> 12:13.157
People with sense belong
wherever they're put.

12:13.240 --> 12:16.153
Can you imagine her traveling
around the world with a camera bum

12:16.240 --> 12:18.311
who never has more than
a week's salary in the bank?

12:18.400 --> 12:21.074
If she was only ordinary.

12:21.840 --> 12:23.433
You never gonna get married?

12:23.520 --> 12:25.989
I'll probably get married one
of these days,

12:26.080 --> 12:28.549
but when I do, it's gonna be
to someone who thinks of life

12:28.640 --> 12:33.271
not just as a new dress
and a lobster dinner

12:33.360 --> 12:35.431
or latest scandal.

12:35.520 --> 12:38.513
I need a woman who's
willing to... Hold it.

12:38.600 --> 12:42.480
Who's willing to go anywhere
and do anything and love it.

12:43.280 --> 12:46.159
So the honest thing for me to do is
just call the whole thing off.

12:46.240 --> 12:48.232
Let her find somebody else.

12:48.320 --> 12:50.232
Yeah, I can hear you now.

12:50.320 --> 12:52.551
"Get out of my life,
you perfectly wonderful woman.

12:52.640 --> 12:54.313
"You're too good for me."

12:54.400 --> 12:56.756
Look, Mr. Jefferies,
I'm not an educated woman,

12:56.880 --> 12:58.599
but I can tell you one thing.

12:58.680 --> 13:02.037
When a man and a woman see
each other and like each other,

13:02.120 --> 13:06.433
they ought to come together, wham,
like a couple of taxis on Broadway,

13:06.520 --> 13:10.753
and not sit around analyzing each other
like two specimens in a bottle.

13:10.840 --> 13:13.958
There's an intelligent way
to approach marriage.

13:14.040 --> 13:15.156
Intelligence.

13:15.240 --> 13:19.200
Nothing has caused the human race
so much trouble as intelligence.

13:19.280 --> 13:22.000
(SCOFFS) Modern marriage.

13:22.080 --> 13:24.640
No, we've progressed
emotionally.

13:24.720 --> 13:26.074
Baloney.

13:26.200 --> 13:29.511
Once it was, "See somebody,
get excited, get married."

13:30.560 --> 13:34.395
Now it's, "Read a lot of books,
fence with a lot of four-syllable words,

13:34.480 --> 13:36.233
"psychoanalyze each other

13:36.320 --> 13:37.976
"until you can't tell
the difference between

13:38.000 --> 13:41.994
"a petting party and
a civil service exam."

13:42.080 --> 13:44.515
People have different
emotional levels...

13:44.600 --> 13:48.480
When I married Myles, we were both
a couple of maladjusted misfits.

13:48.560 --> 13:53.999
We are still maladjusted misfits,
and we have loved every minute of it.

13:54.080 --> 13:55.080
Well, that's fine, Stella.

13:55.160 --> 13:57.152
Now, would you fix me
a sandwich, please?

13:57.240 --> 13:58.469
Yes, I will.

13:58.560 --> 14:01.439
And I'll spread a little common
sense on the bread.

14:01.520 --> 14:04.354
Lisa's loaded to her fingertips
with love for you.

14:04.440 --> 14:07.478
I got two words of advice
for you. Marry her.

14:07.560 --> 14:08.789
She pay you much?

14:08.880 --> 14:09.916
Oh!

14:26.800 --> 14:28.314
There.

14:32.400 --> 14:34.471
- Here's the key.
- Thank you.

14:42.280 --> 14:45.159
Well, if you want
anything, just ring.

14:58.000 --> 15:00.310
Honey. Come on.

15:07.920 --> 15:10.480
Got to carry you
over the threshold.

15:33.520 --> 15:35.113
Window shopper.

15:38.400 --> 15:39.914
(PIANO PLAYING)

15:40.040 --> 15:41.554
(WOMAN SINGING)

16:25.240 --> 16:27.118
How's your leg?

16:27.200 --> 16:28.554
It hurts a little.

16:29.200 --> 16:30.634
And your stomach?

16:30.760 --> 16:32.240
Empty as a football.

16:34.920 --> 16:36.832
And your love life?

16:36.920 --> 16:38.513
Not too active.

16:40.120 --> 16:42.237
Anything else bothering you?

16:42.600 --> 16:43.875
Mmm-hmm.

16:44.520 --> 16:46.113
Who are you?

16:48.600 --> 16:52.640
Reading from top
to bottom, Lisa

16:56.160 --> 16:57.480
Carol

17:01.800 --> 17:03.120
Fremont.

17:04.680 --> 17:08.151
Is this the Lisa Fremont who never wears
the same dress twice?

17:08.240 --> 17:10.675
Only because it's
expected of her.

17:10.800 --> 17:14.510
It's right off the Paris plane.
Do you think it'll sell?

17:14.600 --> 17:17.195
That depends on the quote,
you know. Let's see now.

17:17.280 --> 17:22.036
There's the airplane ticket over and import
duties, hidden taxes, profit markup...

17:22.120 --> 17:24.077
A steal at $1,100.

17:24.160 --> 17:26.516
Eleven hundred?

17:26.600 --> 17:29.035
They ought to list that dress
on the stock exchange.

17:29.120 --> 17:32.158
Why, we sell a dozen
a day in this price range.

17:32.280 --> 17:33.714
Who buys them,
tax collectors?

17:33.800 --> 17:37.271
Even if I had to pay, it would be
worth it. Just for the occasion.

17:37.360 --> 17:39.477
Why, is something big
going on somewhere?

17:39.560 --> 17:42.359
It's going on right here.
It's a big night.

17:42.440 --> 17:47.754
It's just an old run-of-the-mill Wednesday.
The calendar's full of them.

17:47.840 --> 17:52.915
It's opening night of the last depressing week
of L.B. Jefferies in a cast.

17:53.000 --> 17:56.550
Well, I haven't noticed a big demand
for tickets or anything.

17:56.640 --> 17:59.439
That's because I
bought out the house.

17:59.520 --> 18:02.319
You know, this cigarette box has
seen better days.

18:02.400 --> 18:06.952
I picked that up in Shanghai,
which has also seen better days.

18:07.040 --> 18:11.353
It's cracked and you never use it.
It's too ornate.

18:11.440 --> 18:15.320
I'm sending up a plain, flat, silver one
with just your initials engraved.

18:15.400 --> 18:18.040
That's no way to spend your
hard-earned money.

18:18.120 --> 18:19.554
Well, I wanted to.

18:20.360 --> 18:21.396
Oh!

18:23.320 --> 18:26.392
What would you think of starting off
with dinner at '21'?

18:26.480 --> 18:29.040
You have, perhaps, an
ambulance downstairs?

18:29.120 --> 18:32.670
No, better than that. '21.'

18:37.120 --> 18:40.716
Thank you for waiting, Carl.
The kitchen's right there on the left.

18:40.800 --> 18:42.519
Oh, I'll take the wine.

18:45.240 --> 18:47.038
- Good evening, Mr. Jefferies.
- Carl.

18:47.120 --> 18:49.760
Just put everything in the oven,
Carl, on low.

18:49.880 --> 18:51.473
- Yes, madam.
- Let's open the wine now.

18:51.560 --> 18:52.914
JEFF: All right.

18:53.720 --> 18:55.120
It's a Montrachet.

18:55.200 --> 18:58.398
A great big glassful.
There's a corkscrew right over there.

19:00.120 --> 19:01.839
Here, I'll do it.

19:03.600 --> 19:04.600
Big enough?

19:04.680 --> 19:06.160
Yeah, they're fine.

19:06.240 --> 19:10.075
I can't think of anything more boring
or tiresome than what you've been through,

19:10.200 --> 19:12.016
- and the last week must be the hardest.
- Let me, sir.

19:12.040 --> 19:13.599
All right.

19:13.680 --> 19:16.798
Yeah, I want to get this thing off
and get moving.

19:16.880 --> 19:20.396
Well, I'm going to make this
a week you'll never forget.

19:22.920 --> 19:24.912
Fine, thanks.

19:25.000 --> 19:27.276
Just a minute, Carl.

19:27.360 --> 19:29.317
This will take care
of the taxi as well.

19:29.400 --> 19:30.629
Thank you, Miss Fremont.

19:30.720 --> 19:32.200
Have a pleasant dinner,
Mr. Jefferies.

19:32.280 --> 19:34.590
All right. Good night.

19:34.680 --> 19:36.080
Good night.

19:37.880 --> 19:39.837
(SIGHS) What a day I've had.

19:39.920 --> 19:40.920
Are you tired?

19:40.960 --> 19:42.030
Not a bit.

19:42.120 --> 19:44.316
I was all morning
in a sales meeting,

19:44.400 --> 19:47.871
then I had to dash to the Waldorf
for a quick drink with Madame Dufresne,

19:47.960 --> 19:51.158
who's just over from Paris
with some spy reports.

19:52.320 --> 19:56.280
And then I had to go to '21' and have lunch
with the <i>Harper's Bazaar</i> people.

19:56.400 --> 19:58.357
And that's when
I ordered dinner.

19:58.440 --> 20:01.194
Then I had two fall showings
20 blocks apart.

20:01.280 --> 20:03.875
Then I had to have a cocktail
with Leland and Slim Hayward.

20:03.960 --> 20:05.679
We're trying to
get his new show.

20:05.760 --> 20:07.797
And then I had to
dash back and change.

20:07.880 --> 20:12.432
Well, now, tell me. Tell me.
Now, what was Mrs. Hayward wearing?

20:12.560 --> 20:14.392
- Oh, she looked wonderfully cool.
- She did.

20:14.480 --> 20:17.359
She had on the most
divine Italian hand-print

20:17.480 --> 20:19.437
- mousseline de soie...
- Oh, Italian?

20:19.560 --> 20:21.552
- Oh, you.
- Italian. Imagine.

20:21.640 --> 20:25.077
To think I planted three nice items
in the columns about you today.

20:25.200 --> 20:26.200
You did?

20:26.280 --> 20:27.720
You can't buy that
kind of publicity.

20:27.800 --> 20:29.393
I know.

20:29.480 --> 20:33.360
Someday you may want to open up
a studio of your own here.

20:33.440 --> 20:37.116
How would I run it
from, say, Pakistan?

20:37.840 --> 20:39.433
Jeff...

20:40.480 --> 20:44.759
Isn't it time you came home?
You could pick your assignment.

20:45.840 --> 20:47.672
I wish there
was one I wanted.

20:47.760 --> 20:48.989
Make the one you want.

20:49.120 --> 20:50.918
- You mean leave the magazine?
- Yes.

20:51.000 --> 20:52.000
For what?

20:52.040 --> 20:53.918
For yourself and me.

20:55.520 --> 20:57.280
I could get you a dozen
assignments tomorrow.

20:59.480 --> 21:01.358
Don't laugh. I could do it.

21:01.440 --> 21:03.238
That's what I'm afraid of.

21:03.320 --> 21:06.552
Can you see me driving down
to the fashion salon in a jeep,

21:06.640 --> 21:09.758
wearing combat boots
and a three-day beard?

21:09.840 --> 21:11.240
Wouldn't that make a hit?

21:11.360 --> 21:15.991
I could see you looking very handsome
and successful in a dark blue flannel suit.

21:16.080 --> 21:19.630
Let's stop talking
nonsense, shall we?

21:19.720 --> 21:20.836
Hmm?

21:23.440 --> 21:26.433
Guess I'd better start
setting up for dinner.

21:35.800 --> 21:41.319
<i>MAN: (SINGING)
To see you is to love you</i>

21:42.200 --> 21:47.036
<i>And I see you everywhere</i>

21:48.000 --> 21:52.836
<i>In the sunrise
In the moon glow</i>

21:54.040 --> 21:58.114
<i>Any place I look you're there</i>

21:59.760 --> 22:04.915
<i>To see you is to want you</i>

22:05.720 --> 22:10.078
<i>And I see you all the time</i>

22:11.280 --> 22:15.513
<i>On a sidewalk
In a doorway</i>

22:16.760 --> 22:21.437
<i>On the lonely stairs I climb</i>

22:23.240 --> 22:27.519
<i>Someday let me show you</i>

22:27.880 --> 22:32.352
<i>How happy endings start</i>

22:33.120 --> 22:38.593
<i>You'll find how well
I know you</i>

22:39.480 --> 22:43.030
<i>'Cause to know you at all</i>

22:43.120 --> 22:46.750
<i>Is to know you by heart</i>

22:48.000 --> 22:53.792
<i>To see you is to love you</i>

22:54.240 --> 22:59.474
<i>And you're never out of sight</i>

22:59.960 --> 23:05.115
<i>And I love you
and I'll see you</i>

23:06.080 --> 23:10.552
<i>In the same old dream</i>

23:10.640 --> 23:16.113
<i>Tonight</i>

23:30.840 --> 23:32.479
Miss Lonelyheart.

23:32.560 --> 23:35.394
At least that's something
you'll never have to worry about.

23:35.480 --> 23:37.073
Oh?

23:37.160 --> 23:41.040
You can see my apartment from here,
all the way up on 63rd Street?

23:41.120 --> 23:44.272
No, not exactly, but we have
a little apartment here

23:44.400 --> 23:46.596
that's probably about
as popular as yours.

23:46.680 --> 23:50.276
You remember, of course,
Miss Torso, the ballet dancer?

23:51.320 --> 23:54.552
She's like a queen bee
with her pick of the drones.

23:54.800 --> 23:56.678
(ALL CHATTERING)

24:04.240 --> 24:07.995
LISA: I'd say she's doing
a woman's hardest job. Juggling wolves.

24:15.040 --> 24:16.360
Thank you.

24:28.480 --> 24:31.314
Well, she picked the most
prosperous-looking one.

24:31.400 --> 24:34.472
She's not in love with him
or any of them.

24:34.560 --> 24:37.155
Oh, how can you
tell that from here?

24:38.240 --> 24:41.438
You said it resembled
my apartment, didn't you?

24:57.760 --> 25:00.070
I hope they're
cooked this time.

25:11.760 --> 25:13.558
(INAUDIBLE)

25:51.360 --> 25:53.033
(WOMAN CHATTERING)

26:12.920 --> 26:14.479
(PIANO PLAYING)

26:25.560 --> 26:27.916
Where's that wonderful
music coming from?

26:28.000 --> 26:31.232
Some songwriter over there
in the studio apartment.

26:31.360 --> 26:32.714
Wow!

26:32.800 --> 26:36.840
He lives alone. Probably
had a very unhappy marriage.

26:38.560 --> 26:40.358
Oh, it's enchanting.

26:43.640 --> 26:47.270
It's almost as if it were being
written especially for us.

26:47.360 --> 26:48.396
Hmm.

26:48.480 --> 26:51.473
No wonder he's having so much
trouble with it.

26:54.200 --> 26:56.840
At least you can't say
the dinner isn't right.

26:56.920 --> 27:00.038
Lisa, it's perfect.

27:02.120 --> 27:03.520
As always.

27:14.760 --> 27:17.798
There can't be that much difference
between people and the way they live.

27:17.880 --> 27:20.349
We all eat, talk,
drink, laugh, wear clothes.

27:20.440 --> 27:21.874
Well, now look...

27:21.960 --> 27:24.794
If you're saying all this because you don't
want to tell me the truth,

27:24.880 --> 27:27.296
because you're hiding something
from me, then maybe I can understand.

27:27.320 --> 27:28.993
I'm not hiding anything.
It's just that...

27:29.080 --> 27:30.434
It doesn't make sense.

27:30.520 --> 27:33.638
What's so different about it here
from over there or anyplace you go

27:33.720 --> 27:36.315
that one person couldn't live
in both places just as easily?

27:36.400 --> 27:38.056
Some people can.
Now, if you'll just let me...

27:38.080 --> 27:41.391
What is it but traveling from one place
to another, taking pictures?

27:41.480 --> 27:43.676
It's just like being a tourist
on an endless vacation.

27:43.760 --> 27:45.080
Okay.
Now that's your opinion.

27:45.160 --> 27:47.056
You're entitled to it.
Now, let me give you my side.

27:47.080 --> 27:49.117
It's ridiculous to say that
it can only be done

27:49.200 --> 27:52.238
by a special, private little group
of anointed people.

27:52.320 --> 27:55.279
I made a simple statement,
a true statement,

27:55.400 --> 27:58.871
but I can back it up if you'd
just shut up for a minute.

27:58.960 --> 28:02.317
If your opinion is as rude as your manner,
I don't think I care to hear it.

28:02.400 --> 28:03.800
Oh, come on now. Simmer down.

28:03.880 --> 28:07.191
I can't fit in here.
You can't fit in there.

28:07.280 --> 28:10.512
I mean, according to you, people should be born,
live and die on the same spot!

28:10.600 --> 28:11.795
Shut up!

28:14.560 --> 28:16.950
Did you ever eat
fish heads and rice?

28:17.040 --> 28:18.633
Of course not.

28:18.720 --> 28:21.280
Well, you might have
to if you went with me.

28:21.360 --> 28:27.118
Did you ever try to keep warm in a C-54
at 15,000 feet, 20 degrees below zero?

28:27.200 --> 28:30.352
Oh, I do it all the time. Whenever I have
a few minutes after lunch.

28:30.440 --> 28:32.909
Did you ever get shot at?
Did you ever get run over?

28:33.000 --> 28:34.434
Did you ever get
sandbagged at night

28:34.520 --> 28:37.797
because somebody got unfavorable publicity
from your camera?

28:37.880 --> 28:40.679
Did you ever... Those high heels,
they'll be great in the jungle.

28:40.760 --> 28:42.911
And the nylons and
those six-ounce lingerie.

28:43.000 --> 28:44.559
- Three.
- All right, three.

28:44.640 --> 28:48.077
They'll make a big hit in Finland,
just before you freeze to death.

28:48.160 --> 28:51.710
Well, if there's one thing I know,
it's how to wear the proper clothes.

28:51.800 --> 28:52.995
Yeah, yeah.

28:53.080 --> 28:56.596
Well, try and find a raincoat in Brazil,
even when it isn't raining.

28:56.680 --> 29:00.310
Lisa, in this job
you carry one suitcase.

29:00.400 --> 29:02.915
Your home is
the available transportation.

29:03.000 --> 29:05.151
You don't sleep very much.
You bathe less.

29:05.240 --> 29:07.277
And sometimes
the food that you eat

29:07.360 --> 29:11.115
is made from things that you couldn't
even look at when they're alive.

29:11.200 --> 29:14.989
Jeff, you don't have to be deliberately repulsive
just to impress me I'm wrong.

29:15.080 --> 29:18.437
Deliberately repulsive?
I'm just trying to make it sound good.

29:18.520 --> 29:21.752
You just have to face it, Lisa,
you're not meant for that kind of a life.

29:21.840 --> 29:23.559
Few people are.

29:25.400 --> 29:26.880
You're too stubborn
to argue with.

29:26.960 --> 29:30.431
I'm not stubborn.
I'm just truthful.

29:30.520 --> 29:34.400
I know, a lesser man would have told me
it was one long holiday,

29:34.480 --> 29:38.190
and I would have been awakened
to a rude disillusionment.

29:38.280 --> 29:40.556
Oh, well now, wait a minute.
Wait a minute.

29:40.640 --> 29:45.476
If you want to get vicious on this thing,
I'd be very happy to accommodate you.

29:46.880 --> 29:49.759
No, I don't particularly
want that.

29:52.120 --> 29:53.634
So that's it?

29:53.720 --> 29:56.838
You won't stay here,
and I can't go with you.

29:56.920 --> 29:59.037
It would be the wrong thing.

30:01.200 --> 30:05.240
You don't think either one of us
could ever change?

30:05.320 --> 30:07.516
Right now it doesn't seem so.

30:12.600 --> 30:16.389
I'm in love with you. I don't care
what you do for a living.

30:17.720 --> 30:20.360
I'd just like to be
part of it somehow.

30:22.600 --> 30:25.559
It's deflating to find out
the only way I can be part of it

30:25.640 --> 30:28.792
is to take out a subscription
to your magazine.

30:30.320 --> 30:33.119
I guess I'm not the girl
I thought I was.

30:33.760 --> 30:35.200
There's nothing wrong
with you, Lisa.

30:35.280 --> 30:38.273
You've got this town
in the palm of your hand.

30:40.160 --> 30:41.913
Not quite, it seems.

30:44.080 --> 30:45.719
Goodbye, Jeff.

30:45.800 --> 30:46.995
You mean good night.

30:47.080 --> 30:48.480
I mean what I said.

30:48.560 --> 30:51.917
Well, Lisa,
couldn't we just...

30:52.240 --> 30:53.594
Uh...

30:53.960 --> 30:58.079
Couldn't we just keep things
status quo?

31:00.920 --> 31:02.513
Without any future?

31:02.600 --> 31:04.990
When am I gonna
see you again?

31:06.600 --> 31:08.512
Not for a long time.

31:10.440 --> 31:11.760
At least

31:11.840 --> 31:13.877
not until tomorrow night.

31:49.200 --> 31:50.296
(WOMAN SCREAMING)

31:50.320 --> 31:52.118
- (GLASS SHATTERS)
- WOMAN: Don't!

32:02.160 --> 32:03.833
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

32:17.160 --> 32:18.514
Hey! Rain.

32:22.360 --> 32:25.990
Grab that. The rest of the blanket.
Wait, I'll get the clock.

32:26.080 --> 32:27.400
Oh, no!

32:27.840 --> 32:29.354
(ALARM RINGING)

32:30.360 --> 32:31.589
Put it in, now.

32:35.360 --> 32:36.919
Pull on it.

32:37.000 --> 32:38.957
Let me do it.

32:39.040 --> 32:40.713
In you go.

32:40.880 --> 32:41.880
(EXCLAIMS)

34:57.160 --> 35:00.676
No, you can't come in.
It's much too late.

35:00.760 --> 35:05.118
No, no, not now. Get your foot
out of the way. No, I said good night.

36:46.080 --> 36:47.799
What's that supposed to be,
ma'am?

36:47.880 --> 36:49.872
It's called "Hunger."

37:13.320 --> 37:16.199
You'd think the rain would have
cooled things off.

37:16.280 --> 37:17.496
All it did was
make the heat wet.

37:17.520 --> 37:18.556
That's it. That's it.

37:18.640 --> 37:20.632
That's stiff one, right there.
Right there.

37:20.720 --> 37:23.155
You know, the insurance company
would be much happier

37:23.240 --> 37:26.278
if you'd sleep in bed at night instead of
in that wheelchair.

37:26.360 --> 37:27.430
How'd you know?

37:27.520 --> 37:29.193
Eyes are all bloodshot.

37:29.280 --> 37:31.240
Must have been watching out
that window for hours.

37:31.320 --> 37:32.800
Yeah, I was.

37:32.880 --> 37:34.536
What are you gonna do if
one of them catches you?

37:34.560 --> 37:36.074
It depends which one.

37:36.160 --> 37:37.674
Miss Torso, for example...

37:37.760 --> 37:39.911
You keep your mind off her.

37:40.000 --> 37:42.469
She sure is the "Eat,
drink and be merry" girl.

37:42.560 --> 37:45.712
Yeah, she'll wind up fat,
alcoholic and miserable.

37:45.800 --> 37:47.075
Yeah.

37:47.200 --> 37:53.310
Speaking of misery, poor Miss Lonelyheart.
She drank herself to sleep again, alone.

37:53.400 --> 37:55.596
Poor soul. Oh, well.

37:55.680 --> 37:57.672
Maybe one day she'll
find her happiness.

37:57.760 --> 38:00.594
Yeah, and some man
will lose his.

38:00.680 --> 38:04.276
Isn't there anybody in the neighborhood
who could cast an eye in her direction?

38:04.360 --> 38:05.760
I don't know.

38:05.840 --> 38:09.516
It just might be that the salesman
will be available soon.

38:09.760 --> 38:11.479
Oh, him and his wife
splitting up?

38:11.600 --> 38:13.273
I just can't figure it.

38:13.360 --> 38:18.037
He went out several times last night
in the rain, carrying his sample case.

38:18.120 --> 38:19.360
Well, he's a salesman,
isn't he?

38:19.440 --> 38:21.796
What would he be selling
at 3:00 in the morning?

38:21.880 --> 38:23.314
Flashlights.

38:23.400 --> 38:27.553
Luminous dials for watches.
House numbers that light up.

38:27.640 --> 38:30.838
I don't think so.
I don't think so.

38:31.680 --> 38:33.956
I think he was taking something out
of the apartment.

38:34.080 --> 38:35.080
Uh-huh.

38:36.360 --> 38:38.158
His personal effects.

38:39.080 --> 38:41.595
He's gonna run out
on her, the coward.

38:41.680 --> 38:43.160
Yeah.

38:43.240 --> 38:45.914
Sometimes it's worse
to stay than it is to run.

38:46.000 --> 38:50.836
Yeah, well, it takes a particularly low type
of man to do a thing like that.

38:52.440 --> 38:55.638
How about this morning?
Any further developments?

38:56.680 --> 38:58.751
The shades are all
drawn in the apartment.

38:58.880 --> 39:00.872
- In this heat?
- Yeah.

39:00.960 --> 39:02.792
Well, they're up now.

39:08.600 --> 39:10.000
Get back! Get back!

39:10.080 --> 39:11.355
Where do you want me to go?

39:11.440 --> 39:12.776
Come on, get out of sight.
Get out of sight.

39:12.800 --> 39:14.234
What is it?
What's the matter?

39:14.320 --> 39:17.040
That salesman's looking out
of his window. You see?

39:17.120 --> 39:19.077
Get back! He'll see you.

39:19.160 --> 39:21.595
I'm not shy.
I've been looked at before.

39:21.680 --> 39:23.637
That's no ordinary look.

39:25.280 --> 39:27.033
That's the kind
of look a man gives

39:27.120 --> 39:30.272
when he's afraid somebody
might be watching him.

39:41.680 --> 39:43.273
(WHIMPERING)

39:46.960 --> 39:49.429
Get away from there. Get away from there.
He'll be after you.

39:49.520 --> 39:51.716
Go on, into the house.
Into the house.

39:51.800 --> 39:53.280
(SHOOING)

39:54.520 --> 39:57.319
Goodbye, Mr. Jefferies.
See you tomorrow.

39:57.400 --> 39:58.629
Uh-huh.

40:07.160 --> 40:09.356
And don't sleep in
that chair again.

40:09.440 --> 40:10.440
Uh-huh.

40:10.480 --> 40:11.709
"Uh-huh. Uh-huh."

40:11.960 --> 40:13.952
Great conversationalist.

40:16.360 --> 40:17.680
Stella.

40:18.800 --> 40:22.635
Take those binoculars out of the case
and bring them here, will you?

40:22.720 --> 40:25.554
Trouble. I can smell it.

40:26.920 --> 40:29.674
I'll be glad when they crack that cast,
and I can get out of here.

40:29.760 --> 40:30.910
Uh-huh.

43:31.400 --> 43:33.073
(WOMAN WHISTLING)

43:38.320 --> 43:39.595
(BARKING)

43:41.520 --> 43:43.671
(WOMAN WHISTLING MELODY)

44:07.280 --> 44:10.512
How far does a girl have
to go before you'll notice her?

44:10.600 --> 44:14.640
Well, if she's pretty enough,
she doesn't have to go anywhere.

44:14.720 --> 44:16.951
She just has to "be."

44:17.040 --> 44:18.076
Well, ain't I?

44:18.840 --> 44:20.638
Pay attention to me.

44:23.200 --> 44:26.796
I'm not exactly on
the other side of the room.

44:27.240 --> 44:28.833
Your mind is...

44:29.760 --> 44:32.594
And when I want a man,
I want all of him.

44:36.960 --> 44:38.679
Don't you ever
have any problems?

44:38.760 --> 44:40.399
I have one now.

44:41.920 --> 44:43.195
So do I.

44:44.760 --> 44:46.638
Tell me about it.

44:48.920 --> 44:50.115
Why...

44:51.360 --> 44:57.038
Why would a man leave his apartment three times
on a rainy night with a suitcase,

44:57.120 --> 44:59.874
and come back three times?

44:59.960 --> 45:01.792
He likes the way his
wife welcomes him home.

45:01.880 --> 45:03.792
No, no. No, no.

45:03.880 --> 45:06.873
No, no. Not this
salesman's wife.

45:06.960 --> 45:08.952
And why didn't he
go to work today?

45:09.040 --> 45:11.350
Homework. It's
more interesting.

45:14.040 --> 45:17.556
What's interesting
about a butcher knife

45:17.640 --> 45:20.030
and a small saw

45:20.120 --> 45:22.555
wrapped in newspaper? Hmm?

45:22.640 --> 45:25.235
Nothing, thank heaven.

45:25.320 --> 45:28.199
Why hasn't he been
in his wife's bedroom all day?

45:28.320 --> 45:30.437
I wouldn't dare answer that.

45:31.800 --> 45:37.273
Well, listen. I'll answer it, Lisa.
There's something terribly wrong.

45:40.160 --> 45:42.436
And I'm afraid it's with me.

45:42.520 --> 45:43.520
What do you think?

45:45.000 --> 45:47.469
Something too
frightful to utter.

45:49.120 --> 45:53.319
He went out a few minutes ago
in his undershirt, hasn't come back yet.

46:05.680 --> 46:08.673
That'd be a terrible job
to tackle.

46:09.760 --> 46:11.353
(PIANO PLAYING)

46:14.640 --> 46:17.712
Just how would you start
to cut up a human body?

46:20.200 --> 46:24.399
Jeff, I'll be honest with you.
You're beginning to scare me a little.

46:26.880 --> 46:29.176
Jeff, did you hear what I said?
You're beginning to scare...

46:29.200 --> 46:31.192
Shh! Shh!

46:31.280 --> 46:32.999
He's coming back!

47:00.880 --> 47:02.758
Jeff, if you could
only see yourself!

47:02.880 --> 47:03.880
What's the matter?

47:03.960 --> 47:06.576
Sitting around looking out of the window
to kill time is one thing,

47:06.600 --> 47:10.719
but doing it the way you are,
with binoculars and wild opinions

47:10.800 --> 47:13.395
about every little thing you see,
is diseased!

47:13.480 --> 47:16.154
What, do you think I
consider it recreation?

47:16.240 --> 47:17.993
I don't know what
you consider it,

47:18.080 --> 47:20.151
but if you don't stop it,
I'm getting out of here.

47:20.240 --> 47:21.310
What's the...

47:21.440 --> 47:22.794
What is it you're
looking for?

47:22.880 --> 47:25.736
I just want to find out what's the matter
with the salesman's wife, that's all.

47:25.760 --> 47:27.433
Does that make me
sound like a madman?

47:27.520 --> 47:29.416
What makes you think there's something
the matter with her?

47:29.440 --> 47:32.990
A lot of things. She's an invalid.
She demands constant care.

47:33.120 --> 47:37.353
Yet neither the husband or anybody else
has been in to see her all day. Why?

47:39.120 --> 47:40.440
Maybe she died.

47:40.520 --> 47:43.194
Where's the doctor?
Where's the undertaker?

47:43.280 --> 47:46.159
She could be sleeping,
under sedatives.

47:46.240 --> 47:49.074
He's in there now.
There's nothing to see.

47:49.160 --> 47:51.675
There is something...
I've seen it through that window.

47:51.760 --> 47:54.594
I've seen bickering
and family quarrels

47:54.680 --> 47:57.912
and mysterious trips at night
and knives and saws and ropes.

47:58.000 --> 47:59.936
And now, since last evening,
not a sign of the wife.

47:59.960 --> 48:01.456
All right. Now you tell me
where she is.

48:01.480 --> 48:03.400
- I don't know.
- What's she doing? Where is she?

48:03.480 --> 48:06.439
Maybe he's leaving his wife.
I don't know. I don't care!

48:06.520 --> 48:09.638
Lots of people have knives and saws
and ropes around their houses.

48:09.720 --> 48:12.679
And lots of men don't speak
to their wives all day.

48:12.760 --> 48:15.355
Lots of wives nag and men hate them
and trouble starts,

48:15.440 --> 48:19.559
but very, very few of them end up in murder,
if that's what you're thinking.

48:19.640 --> 48:22.096
It's pretty hard for you to keep away
from that word, isn't it?

48:22.120 --> 48:24.760
- You could see all that he did, couldn't you?
- Of course...

48:24.880 --> 48:26.473
You could see
because the shades were up

48:26.560 --> 48:29.871
and he walked along the corridor
and the street and the backyard.

48:29.960 --> 48:31.376
I've seen him in the backyard,
fixing the flowers...

48:31.400 --> 48:34.393
Oh, Jeff, do you think a murderer
would let you see all that?

48:34.480 --> 48:37.200
That he wouldn't pull the shades down
and hide behind them?

48:37.280 --> 48:39.496
Just where he's being clever.
He's being nonchalant about it.

48:39.520 --> 48:41.318
And that's where you're not
being clever.

48:41.400 --> 48:45.235
A murderer would never parade his crime
in front of an open window.

48:45.320 --> 48:46.436
Why not?

48:46.520 --> 48:47.520
Why, for all you know,

48:47.600 --> 48:50.752
there's probably something a lot more
sinister going on behind those windows.

48:50.840 --> 48:52.069
Where?

48:52.160 --> 48:53.276
Oh!

48:54.280 --> 48:55.634
(CHUCKLES)

48:55.800 --> 48:57.234
No comment.

49:19.840 --> 49:23.390
Let's start from
the beginning again, Jeff.

49:23.520 --> 49:26.877
Tell me everything you saw

49:26.960 --> 49:29.759
and what you think it means.

49:41.080 --> 49:42.150
(PHONE RINGS)

49:42.240 --> 49:43.240
Yeah?

49:43.320 --> 49:44.456
<i>LISA: The name
on the second floor</i>

49:44.480 --> 49:47.996
<i>rear mail box reads,
"Mr. and Mrs. Lars."</i>

49:48.080 --> 49:50.436
<i>That's L-A-R-S.</i>

49:50.520 --> 49:52.477
<i>Lars Thorwald.</i>

49:52.560 --> 49:54.233
What's the number of
the apartment house?

49:54.320 --> 49:56.880
<i>125 West 9th Street.</i>

49:58.080 --> 49:59.480
Thank you, dear.

49:59.600 --> 50:01.831
<i>Okay, chief.
What's my next assignment?</i>

50:01.920 --> 50:02.920
Just go home.

50:03.840 --> 50:06.719
<i>All right,
but what's he doing now?</i>

50:06.800 --> 50:10.237
He's just sitting
in the living room in the dark.

50:11.040 --> 50:13.600
Hasn't gone near the bedroom.

50:13.680 --> 50:15.353
Now you go home
and get some sleep.

50:15.440 --> 50:17.397
- Good night.
<i>- Good night.</i>

50:27.280 --> 50:28.616
<i>DOYLE: All right.
What's it all about, Jeff?</i>

50:28.640 --> 50:31.997
Now look, Doyle, it's just something
I can't tell you over the telephone.

50:32.080 --> 50:33.958
You have to be here
and see the whole setup.

50:34.040 --> 50:35.040
<i>Important, huh?</i>

50:35.120 --> 50:36.696
Well, it's probably
nothing important at all.

50:36.720 --> 50:38.791
It's just a little neighborhood murder,
that's all.

50:38.880 --> 50:39.920
<i>Did you say "murder," Jeff?</i>

50:39.960 --> 50:41.656
Yes, as a matter of fact,
I did say "murder."

50:41.680 --> 50:43.034
<i>Oh, come now.</i>

50:43.120 --> 50:46.477
My only thought was throwing
a little business your way, that's all.

50:46.560 --> 50:49.520
I figured a good detective would jump
at the chance of something to detect.

50:49.640 --> 50:51.199
<i>- I'm not working.</i>
- What?

50:51.280 --> 50:52.953
<i>This happens to
be my day off.</i>

50:53.040 --> 50:55.999
Well, I usually took
my best pictures on my day off.

50:56.120 --> 50:57.156
<i>I'll drop by.</i>

50:57.280 --> 50:59.875
Okay, Doyle. All right.
As soon as you can.

51:01.320 --> 51:05.109
Bless your heart, Stella.
Gee whiz, look at this.

51:05.200 --> 51:07.157
I can't tell you
what a welcome sight this is.

51:07.240 --> 51:09.232
No wonder your husband
still loves you.

51:09.320 --> 51:10.913
- The police.
- What?

51:11.000 --> 51:12.000
You called the police.

51:12.080 --> 51:13.992
Well, not exactly.

51:14.080 --> 51:17.790
Not an official call.
It's just an old friend of mine.

51:17.880 --> 51:20.918
An old, ornery
friend of mine.

51:21.000 --> 51:23.834
Just where do you
suppose he cut her up?

51:25.480 --> 51:27.995
Of course. The bathtub.

51:28.880 --> 51:32.920
That's the only place where he could
have washed away the blood.

51:41.800 --> 51:45.680
He better get that trunk out
of there before it starts to leak.

52:07.200 --> 52:08.634
WOMAN: Harry?

52:14.000 --> 52:16.037
Look, look, Mr. Jefferies.

52:53.760 --> 52:56.000
I thought Doyle would be here
by the time the trunk left,

52:56.080 --> 52:58.549
or I'd have called the police.
Now we're gonna lose it.

52:58.680 --> 53:00.296
- Hold everything.
- Don't do anything foolish.

53:00.320 --> 53:02.216
I'm just gonna get the name off
that freight truck.

53:02.240 --> 53:04.471
I'll keep an eye
on the alley.

53:18.040 --> 53:19.633
Long distance.

53:44.800 --> 53:48.396
You didn't see the killing or the body.
How do you know there was a murder?

53:48.480 --> 53:51.518
Because everything this fellow's done
has been suspicious.

53:51.600 --> 53:55.355
Trips at night in the rain, and knives
and saws and trunks with rope

53:55.440 --> 53:57.671
and now this wife
that isn't there anymore.

53:57.760 --> 54:02.551
I admit it all has a mysterious sound.
It could be any number of things.

54:02.640 --> 54:03.994
Murder's the least plausible.

54:04.080 --> 54:06.470
Doyle, don't tell me
he's an unemployed magician,

54:06.560 --> 54:09.598
amusing the neighborhood with his sleight
of hand. Don't tell me that.

54:09.680 --> 54:12.070
It's too obvious and stupid
a way to commit murder.

54:12.160 --> 54:13.799
In full view of 50 windows?

54:13.880 --> 54:15.136
Then sit over there
smoking a cigar,

54:15.160 --> 54:16.816
waiting for the police
to come and pick him up?

54:16.840 --> 54:19.230
All right, Officer, do your duty.
Go over and pick him up.

54:19.360 --> 54:22.592
Jeff, you've got a lot to learn
about homicide.

54:22.680 --> 54:24.876
Why, morons have
committed murder so shrewdly,

54:24.960 --> 54:28.237
it's taken a hundred trained police minds
to catch them.

54:28.320 --> 54:30.915
That salesman wouldn't just knock
his wife off after dinner

54:31.000 --> 54:33.515
and toss her in the trunk
and put her in storage.

54:33.600 --> 54:34.920
I'll bet it's been done.

54:35.040 --> 54:38.238
Most everything's
been done, under panic.

54:38.320 --> 54:40.391
This is
a thousand-to-one shot.

54:40.480 --> 54:43.791
He's still sitting around the apartment.
That man's not panicked.

54:43.880 --> 54:45.951
You think I made all this up?

54:46.040 --> 54:47.952
Well, I think
you saw something.

54:48.040 --> 54:50.680
There's probably a very simple
explanation for that.

54:50.760 --> 54:52.274
For instance?

54:52.360 --> 54:53.919
Wife took a trip.

54:54.000 --> 54:55.992
His wife was sick in bed.

54:57.480 --> 54:59.233
Yeah, so you told me.

55:00.840 --> 55:03.309
Well, Jeff,
I've got to run along.

55:04.560 --> 55:09.794
I won't report this to the department.
Let me poke into it a little on my own.

55:09.880 --> 55:12.634
No sense in your getting a lot of
ridiculous publicity.

55:12.720 --> 55:14.074
Thank you.

55:16.280 --> 55:19.239
We know the wife is gone,
so I'll see if I can find out where.

55:19.320 --> 55:20.549
Do that.

55:21.760 --> 55:24.275
You had any headaches lately?

55:24.360 --> 55:25.714
Not until you showed up.

55:26.800 --> 55:29.793
It'll wear off,
along with the hallucinations.

55:29.880 --> 55:31.439
See you around.

55:34.360 --> 55:35.874
(DOG WHINING)

55:55.840 --> 55:57.320
Get along.

55:57.400 --> 55:58.754
(DOG BARKING)

56:18.600 --> 56:20.080
He has a six months' lease.

56:20.160 --> 56:23.119
Used up a little more than five
and a half months of it.

56:23.200 --> 56:25.635
He's quiet, drinks,
but not to drunkenness.

56:25.720 --> 56:28.633
Pays his bills promptly with money earned
as a costume jewelry salesman.

56:28.720 --> 56:30.040
Wholesale.

56:30.320 --> 56:33.438
Kept to himself. None of his neighbors
got close to him or his wife.

56:33.520 --> 56:35.416
Yeah, well, I think they missed
their chance with her.

56:35.440 --> 56:36.456
She never left
the apartment...

56:36.480 --> 56:38.376
- Where is she? The icebox?
- ...until yesterday morning.

56:38.400 --> 56:40.710
- What time?
- 6:00 a.m.

56:40.800 --> 56:42.154
6:00 a.m.

56:43.200 --> 56:45.999
I think that's about
the time I fell asleep.

56:46.080 --> 56:47.309
Too bad.

56:47.640 --> 56:51.475
Thorwalds were leaving their apartment
at just that time.

56:52.520 --> 56:54.512
Feel a little foolish?

56:54.960 --> 56:56.474
No, not yet.

57:08.120 --> 57:09.918
How's your wife?

57:10.000 --> 57:11.150
Oh, she's fine.

57:14.840 --> 57:16.877
Who said they left then?

57:17.520 --> 57:18.520
Who left where?

57:18.600 --> 57:20.671
The Thorwalds,
at 6:00 in the morning.

57:20.760 --> 57:23.434
Oh, the building superintendent
and two tenants.

57:23.520 --> 57:26.558
Flat statements, no hesitation.
They jived with the letter.

57:26.640 --> 57:29.075
Thorwalds were on their way
to the railroad station.

57:29.160 --> 57:32.676
Well, now, Tom, how could anybody
possibly guess that?

57:32.800 --> 57:36.919
What, did they have signs on their luggage,
saying, "Grand Central or bust?"

57:37.000 --> 57:39.754
The superintendent met Thorwald
on his way back.

57:39.840 --> 57:42.958
Thorwald told him that he'd put
his wife on a train to the country.

57:43.040 --> 57:46.192
I see. I'd say this is a pretty
convenient guy, this superintendent.

57:46.280 --> 57:49.193
Have you checked his bank
statements lately?

57:50.400 --> 57:51.470
Huh?

57:51.560 --> 57:54.075
Well, what good's
his information?

57:54.160 --> 57:56.550
It's a secondhand version
of an unsupported story

57:56.640 --> 57:58.393
by the murderer
himself, Thorwald.

57:58.480 --> 58:02.030
Now did anybody actually see
the wife get on the train?

58:03.040 --> 58:04.040
I hate to remind you,

58:04.080 --> 58:06.834
but this all started because you said
she was murdered.

58:06.960 --> 58:10.078
Now, did anyone, including you,
actually see her murdered?

58:10.160 --> 58:11.992
What are you doing?

58:12.080 --> 58:16.120
Are you interested in solving this case
or in making me look foolish?

58:16.200 --> 58:18.317
Well, if possible, both.

58:18.400 --> 58:19.914
Well, then, do a
good job of it.

58:20.000 --> 58:21.878
Go over there
and search Thorwald's apartment.

58:21.960 --> 58:23.838
It must be knee-deep
in evidence.

58:23.920 --> 58:25.354
I can't do that.

58:25.480 --> 58:27.776
Well, no, I don't mean right now,
but when he goes out for a drink,

58:27.800 --> 58:30.096
or a paper, or something.
What he doesn't know won't hurt him.

58:30.120 --> 58:32.510
I can't do that,
even if he isn't there.

58:32.600 --> 58:37.356
What's he... Does he have a courtesy card
from the police department, or something?

58:37.440 --> 58:39.272
Now don't get me mad.

58:39.480 --> 58:42.598
Not even a detective can walk
into an apartment and search it.

58:42.680 --> 58:45.752
If I were caught in there,
they'd have my badge within 10 minutes.

58:45.840 --> 58:48.355
All right. Make sure
you don't get caught, that's all.

58:48.440 --> 58:50.671
What? If you find something,
you've got a murderer,

58:50.760 --> 58:52.936
and they don't care anything
about a couple of house rules.

58:52.960 --> 58:55.031
If you don't find anything,
the fellow's clear.

58:55.120 --> 58:59.512
At the risk of sounding stuffy, I'd like
to remind you of the Constitution

58:59.640 --> 59:02.474
and the phrase, "Search warrant issued
by a judge

59:02.600 --> 59:04.637
"who knows his Bill
of Rights verbatim."

59:04.720 --> 59:06.279
He must ask for evidence.

59:06.360 --> 59:07.714
Give him evidence.

59:09.600 --> 59:11.512
Yeah, I can hear myself.

59:11.600 --> 59:15.640
"Your Honor, I have a friend
who's an amateur sleuth.

59:16.640 --> 59:20.395
"Well, the other night after having
a heavy dinner, he..."

59:20.480 --> 59:22.915
He'd throw the New York State
penal code right in my face.

59:23.000 --> 59:25.071
And there's six volumes.

59:25.800 --> 59:28.520
You know, by tomorrow morning
there may not be any evidence left

59:28.600 --> 59:30.717
over in that apartment.
You know that.

59:30.800 --> 59:33.110
It's a detective's nightmare.

59:33.520 --> 59:35.910
What do you need
before you can search?

59:36.000 --> 59:37.200
Tell me. Now,
what do you need?

59:37.280 --> 59:39.795
Bloody footsteps leading up
to the door?

59:39.880 --> 59:43.510
One thing I don't need is heckling.
You called me and asked for help.

59:43.640 --> 59:46.155
Now you're behaving
like a taxpayer.

59:47.000 --> 59:48.912
How did we ever stand
each other in that plane

59:49.000 --> 59:50.992
for three years
during the war?

59:53.360 --> 59:56.320
I guess I'll go over to the railroad station
and check on Thorwald's story.

59:56.400 --> 59:58.073
Oh, forget about the story.

59:58.160 --> 01:00:00.834
Find the trunk.
Mrs. Thorwald's in it.

01:00:01.240 --> 01:00:02.435
Oh, I almost forgot.

01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:05.877
There was a postcard in
Thorwald's mailbox. It was

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:09.159
mailed 3:30 yesterday
afternoon from Merritsville.

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:11.311
That's 80 miles
north of here.

01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:15.189
Message went, "Arrived okay.
Already feeling better.

01:00:15.280 --> 01:00:16.714
"Love, Anna."

01:00:19.480 --> 01:00:20.550
Oh...

01:00:20.640 --> 01:00:22.040
Is that...

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:25.469
Anna... Is that
who I think it is?

01:00:26.720 --> 01:00:28.200
Mrs. Thorwald.

01:00:31.680 --> 01:00:33.990
Oh, so anything
you need, Jeff?

01:00:34.400 --> 01:00:36.915
You might send me
a good detective.

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:49.789
(SIGHS)

01:01:03.400 --> 01:01:06.120
(WOMAN WHISTLING MELODY)

01:02:30.680 --> 01:02:32.319
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

01:02:46.400 --> 01:02:48.198
(CHATTERING)

01:02:48.680 --> 01:02:52.879
<i>WOMAN: (SINGING)
Many dreams ago</i>

01:02:53.880 --> 01:02:58.352
<i>I dreamed many dreams</i>

01:03:00.040 --> 01:03:05.559
<i>Waiting for my true love</i>

01:03:05.640 --> 01:03:09.953
<i>To appear</i>

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:16.598
<i>Though each night it seems</i>

01:03:16.920 --> 01:03:22.234
- (BOTH CHATTERING)
<i>- Someone filled my dreams</i>

01:03:22.680 --> 01:03:25.070
- (CAR HORN BLARING)
<i>- Still the face</i>

01:03:25.160 --> 01:03:30.554
<i>That I kissed wasn't clear</i>

01:03:33.240 --> 01:03:39.111
<i>So I let my heart dance</i>

01:03:39.560 --> 01:03:44.555
<i>To many sweet refrains</i>

01:03:44.760 --> 01:03:49.755
<i>But now just one song</i>

01:03:50.720 --> 01:03:55.920
<i>And just one dream remains</i>

01:03:56.120 --> 01:04:00.114
<i>That one song is you</i>

01:04:01.600 --> 01:04:05.992
<i>And at last I know</i>

01:04:06.080 --> 01:04:10.916
<i>You're the one I dreamed of</i>

01:04:11.240 --> 01:04:15.439
<i>Many dreams ago</i>

01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:25.993
<i>- MRS. DOYLE: Hello?</i>
- Hello, Miss Doyle?

01:04:26.080 --> 01:04:27.080
<i>Yes?</i>

01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:28.680
This is Jeff again.
Has Tom come in yet?

01:04:28.800 --> 01:04:29.800
<i>Not yet, Jeff.</i>

01:04:29.840 --> 01:04:31.832
You mean you haven't
even heard from him?

01:04:31.920 --> 01:04:35.391
<i>Not a word. Is it something
really important, Jeff?</i>

01:04:35.480 --> 01:04:37.153
Yeah, I'm afraid it is, Tess.

01:04:37.240 --> 01:04:39.232
<i>I'll have him call the moment
I hear from him.</i>

01:04:39.320 --> 01:04:40.720
No, no, don't
bother to do that.

01:04:40.800 --> 01:04:43.395
Just have him get over here
as soon as he can.

01:04:43.480 --> 01:04:45.153
Looks like Thorwald's
pulling out tonight.

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:46.240
<i>Who's Thorwald?</i>

01:04:46.280 --> 01:04:47.430
He'll know, he'll know.

01:04:47.520 --> 01:04:50.240
And Thorwald's a man,
Tess, don't worry.

01:04:50.320 --> 01:04:52.676
<i>- (CHUCKLES) Good night, you idiot.</i>
- Good night.

01:05:09.440 --> 01:05:11.272
Long distance again.

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:13.514
(INAUDIBLE)

01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:49.156
MAN: There's somebody
at the door.

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:51.152
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:55.511
- Hi.
- Hello.

01:06:17.720 --> 01:06:19.040
Hello.

01:06:22.400 --> 01:06:23.697
What'd you do to your hair?

01:06:23.721 --> 01:06:25.536
- Oh, well, I just sort of...
- Take a look at Thorwald.

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:29.031
He's getting ready to pull out
for good. Look at him.

01:06:32.240 --> 01:06:33.993
He doesn't seem
to be in any hurry.

01:06:34.080 --> 01:06:36.549
He's been laying out all his things
on one of the beds.

01:06:36.680 --> 01:06:38.911
Shirts, suits, coats, socks.

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:41.117
Even his wife's...
That alligator handbag

01:06:41.240 --> 01:06:43.550
- his wife had on the bedpost.
- What about it?

01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:46.360
He had it hidden in the dresser.
At least, it was there.

01:06:46.480 --> 01:06:50.394
He took it out, went to the telephone
and made a long-distance call.

01:06:50.480 --> 01:06:53.154
He had his wife's jewelry
in the handbag.

01:06:53.240 --> 01:06:54.310
Seemed worried about it.

01:06:54.400 --> 01:06:56.153
Asked somebody's advice over
the telephone.

01:06:56.280 --> 01:06:57.873
Someone not his wife.

01:06:57.960 --> 01:07:00.156
Well, I never saw
him ask her for advice.

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:04.075
She volunteered plenty, but I never
saw him ask her for any.

01:07:14.440 --> 01:07:15.840
I wonder where he's going.

01:07:15.920 --> 01:07:17.240
I don't know.

01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:19.719
Suppose he doesn't come
back again?

01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:22.395
He'll be back.
His things are still there.

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:25.600
Well, I guess it's safe
to put on some lights now.

01:07:25.640 --> 01:07:27.120
No, not yet!

01:07:34.120 --> 01:07:38.558
Okay, you can turn it on now.
He must have gone someplace to the right.

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:42.991
All day long, I've been trying
to keep my mind on my work.

01:07:43.120 --> 01:07:44.713
Thinking about Thorwald?

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:46.996
And you and your friend,
Doyle.

01:07:47.480 --> 01:07:49.472
Did you hear from him again,
since he left?

01:07:49.560 --> 01:07:50.560
Not a word.

01:07:50.640 --> 01:07:53.235
He said he was gonna check
the railroad station and the trunk.

01:07:53.320 --> 01:07:55.198
He must be still at it.

01:07:55.880 --> 01:07:57.837
Something on your mind?

01:07:58.520 --> 01:08:00.079
It doesn't make sense to me.

01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:01.594
What doesn't?

01:08:02.080 --> 01:08:04.470
Women aren't that
unpredictable.

01:08:04.960 --> 01:08:05.996
Mmm.

01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:09.198
Well, I can't guess
what you're thinking.

01:08:09.280 --> 01:08:11.670
A woman has a
favorite handbag

01:08:11.760 --> 01:08:15.879
and it always hangs on her bedpost,
where she can get at it easily.

01:08:15.960 --> 01:08:19.954
And then, all of a sudden, she goes away
on a trip and leaves it behind. Why?

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:21.776
Because she didn't know
she was going on a trip.

01:08:21.800 --> 01:08:23.856
And where she's going,
she wouldn't need the handbag.

01:08:23.880 --> 01:08:26.759
Yes, but only her husband
would know that.

01:08:28.040 --> 01:08:29.269
And that jewelry.

01:08:29.360 --> 01:08:31.158
Women don't keep their
jewelry in a purse

01:08:31.240 --> 01:08:33.709
getting all twisted and
scratched and tangled up.

01:08:33.800 --> 01:08:35.456
Well, do they hide it
in their husband's clothes?

01:08:35.480 --> 01:08:37.073
They do not.

01:08:37.160 --> 01:08:39.880
And they don't leave it
behind, either.

01:08:40.280 --> 01:08:42.317
Why, a woman going anywhere
but the hospital

01:08:42.400 --> 01:08:45.393
would always take makeup,
perfume and jewelry.

01:08:47.000 --> 01:08:48.878
Put that over there.
That's inside stuff, huh?

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:50.599
It's basic equipment.

01:08:50.680 --> 01:08:53.559
And you don't leave it behind
in your husband's drawer

01:08:53.640 --> 01:08:55.074
in your favorite handbag.

01:08:55.160 --> 01:08:57.470
Well, I'm with you, sweetie.
I'm with you.

01:08:57.560 --> 01:08:59.517
Tom Doyle has a pat answer
for that, though.

01:08:59.600 --> 01:09:01.557
That Mrs. Thorwald
left at 6:00 a.m.

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:02.710
yesterday with her husband?

01:09:02.800 --> 01:09:04.473
According to those witnesses.

01:09:04.560 --> 01:09:06.791
Well, I have a pat
rebuttal for Mr. Doyle.

01:09:06.880 --> 01:09:10.715
That couldn't have been Mrs. Thorwald,
or I don't know women.

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:12.837
What about the witnesses?

01:09:12.960 --> 01:09:15.191
We'll agree they saw a woman,

01:09:15.720 --> 01:09:17.996
but she was not
Mrs. Thorwald.

01:09:18.360 --> 01:09:20.636
That is, not yet.

01:09:20.760 --> 01:09:21.760
Is that so?

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:27.320
Come here.

01:09:30.760 --> 01:09:34.151
I'd like to see your friend's face
when we tell him.

01:09:34.240 --> 01:09:36.471
He doesn't sound like
much of a detective.

01:09:36.560 --> 01:09:39.997
Oh, don't be too hard on him.
He's a steady worker.

01:09:41.480 --> 01:09:43.597
I sure wish he'd show up.

01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:46.275
Don't rush him.
We have all night.

01:09:48.840 --> 01:09:50.877
- We have all what?
- Night.

01:09:51.440 --> 01:09:53.591
I'm going to stay with you.

01:09:55.560 --> 01:09:58.837
Well, you'll have to clear that
with my landlord.

01:09:59.720 --> 01:10:01.951
I have the whole weekend off.

01:10:02.920 --> 01:10:05.833
That's very nice,
but I just have one bed.

01:10:06.520 --> 01:10:10.150
If you say anything else,
I'll stay tomorrow night, too.

01:10:11.080 --> 01:10:14.118
I won't be able to
give you any pajamas.

01:10:19.560 --> 01:10:22.678
You said I'll have to
live out of one suitcase.

01:10:25.040 --> 01:10:27.077
I'll bet yours
isn't this small.

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:28.793
This is a suitcase?

01:10:28.880 --> 01:10:31.793
Well, a Mark Cross
overnight case, anyway.

01:10:32.280 --> 01:10:35.398
Compact, but ample enough.

01:10:35.480 --> 01:10:37.711
Looks like you
packed in a hurry.

01:10:37.800 --> 01:10:40.315
Look at this.
Isn't that amazing?

01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:42.799
I'll trade you...

01:10:42.880 --> 01:10:45.952
My feminine intuition
for a bed for the night.

01:10:46.040 --> 01:10:48.111
I'll go along with that.

01:10:54.440 --> 01:10:55.999
(PIANO PLAYING)

01:11:01.920 --> 01:11:04.037
There's that song again.

01:11:04.120 --> 01:11:05.759
(ALL CHATTERING)

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:14.356
Where does a man get inspiration
to write a song like that?

01:11:14.440 --> 01:11:17.512
Well, he gets it from the landlady
once a month.

01:11:20.600 --> 01:11:22.557
It's utterly beautiful.

01:11:23.400 --> 01:11:25.073
I wish I could be creative.

01:11:25.160 --> 01:11:26.879
Oh, sweetie, you are.

01:11:27.800 --> 01:11:31.271
You have a great talent
for creating difficult situations.

01:11:31.360 --> 01:11:32.680
- I do?
- Sure.

01:11:32.760 --> 01:11:35.116
Like staying here
all night uninvited.

01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:38.557
Surprise is the most important
element of attack.

01:11:38.640 --> 01:11:41.758
And besides, you're not up
on your private eye literature.

01:11:41.840 --> 01:11:43.960
When they're in trouble,
it's always their Girl Friday

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:45.394
who gets them out of it.

01:11:45.480 --> 01:11:47.631
Is she the girl that saves them
from the clutches

01:11:47.720 --> 01:11:49.200
of the seductive showgirls

01:11:49.280 --> 01:11:51.476
and the over-passionate
daughters of the rich?

01:11:51.560 --> 01:11:53.631
- The same.
- That's the one, huh?

01:11:53.720 --> 01:11:57.475
It's funny. He never ends up
marrying her, does he?

01:11:57.560 --> 01:11:58.710
That's strange.

01:11:58.800 --> 01:11:59.950
Weird.

01:12:03.080 --> 01:12:05.276
Why don't I slip into something
more comfortable?

01:12:05.360 --> 01:12:07.079
By all means.

01:12:07.160 --> 01:12:09.994
I mean, like the kitchen
and make us some coffee.

01:12:10.080 --> 01:12:12.549
Oh, and some
brandy, too, huh?

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:23.674
WOMAN: Harry.

01:12:33.080 --> 01:12:34.480
- Jeff.
- Hi.

01:12:37.920 --> 01:12:39.479
(LISA HUMMING)

01:12:59.440 --> 01:13:01.238
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

01:13:26.960 --> 01:13:28.713
What else have you got
on this man Thorwald?

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:32.231
Well, enough to scare me you wouldn't
show up in time and we'd lose him.

01:13:32.320 --> 01:13:33.336
Think he's getting
out of here?

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:34.840
Look, he's got
everything he owns

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:38.470
laid out over there in the bedroom,
waiting to be packed.

01:13:40.360 --> 01:13:43.990
I'm just warming some brandy.
Mr. Doyle, I presume?

01:13:46.640 --> 01:13:48.757
JEFF: Tom, this is
Miss Lisa Fremont.

01:13:48.840 --> 01:13:49.840
How do you do?

01:13:50.680 --> 01:13:52.876
We think Thorwald's guilty.

01:13:59.840 --> 01:14:01.513
Careful, Tom.

01:14:01.880 --> 01:14:03.473
(PHONE RINGING)

01:14:07.000 --> 01:14:08.096
- Hello.
<i>- MAN: Lieutenant Doyle.</i>

01:14:08.120 --> 01:14:10.476
Yeah, he's right here.
For you.

01:14:11.360 --> 01:14:13.397
- Hello.
<i>- Lieutenant Doyle, sir?</i>

01:14:13.480 --> 01:14:14.800
Speaking.

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:17.160
Yeah.

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:20.554
All right.

01:14:20.880 --> 01:14:23.111
I see. Thank you. Goodbye.

01:14:23.640 --> 01:14:25.791
Coffee will be ready soon.

01:14:25.880 --> 01:14:28.634
Jeff, aren't you going to tell him
about the jewelry?

01:14:28.720 --> 01:14:29.756
Jewelry?

01:14:29.840 --> 01:14:33.197
He's got his wife's jewelry hidden
in his clothes in the bedroom over there.

01:14:33.320 --> 01:14:34.496
You sure it belonged
to his wife?

01:14:34.520 --> 01:14:36.557
It was
in her favorite handbag.

01:14:36.640 --> 01:14:39.314
Mr. Doyle, that can only lead
to one conclusion.

01:14:39.400 --> 01:14:40.436
Namely?

01:14:40.520 --> 01:14:44.480
That it was not Mrs. Thorwald that left
with him yesterday morning.

01:14:44.560 --> 01:14:45.994
You figured that out, eh?

01:14:46.080 --> 01:14:48.515
Well, it's simply that women don't leave
their jewelry behind

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:50.592
when they go on a trip.

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:51.920
Come on, come on, Tom.

01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:55.596
Now you don't really need any
of this information, do you?

01:15:01.480 --> 01:15:03.756
As a matter of fact, I don't.

01:15:09.640 --> 01:15:12.917
Lars Thorwald is no more
a murderer than I am.

01:15:16.880 --> 01:15:19.576
You mean to say you can explain everything
that's gone on over there,

01:15:19.600 --> 01:15:20.875
and is still going on?

01:15:20.960 --> 01:15:22.838
No, neither can you.

01:15:22.920 --> 01:15:25.913
That's a secret, private world
you're looking into out there.

01:15:26.040 --> 01:15:29.056
People do a lot of things in private
they couldn't possibly explain in public.

01:15:29.080 --> 01:15:30.958
Like disposing
of their wives?

01:15:31.040 --> 01:15:33.856
You get that idea out of your mind.
It'll only lead in the wrong direction.

01:15:33.880 --> 01:15:36.315
What about the knife
and the saw?

01:15:36.400 --> 01:15:37.993
Did you ever own a saw?

01:15:38.080 --> 01:15:40.754
Well, at home
in the garage I had...

01:15:40.840 --> 01:15:43.116
How many people
did you cut up with it?

01:15:43.200 --> 01:15:47.638
Or with the couple of hundred knives
you've probably owned in your lifetime?

01:15:48.360 --> 01:15:50.033
Well, your logic is backward.

01:15:50.120 --> 01:15:54.000
You can't ignore the wife disappearing
and the trunk and the jewelry.

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:56.834
I checked the railroad station.
He bought a ticket.

01:15:56.920 --> 01:15:58.877
Ten minutes later,
he put his wife on a train.

01:15:58.960 --> 01:16:02.078
Destination, Merritsville.
The witnesses are that deep.

01:16:02.200 --> 01:16:05.238
That might have been a woman,
but it couldn't have been Mrs. Thorwald.

01:16:05.320 --> 01:16:06.840
- That jewelry...
- Look, Miss Fremont.

01:16:06.920 --> 01:16:09.719
That feminine intuition
stuff sells magazines.

01:16:09.800 --> 01:16:12.759
But in real life,
it's still a fairy tale.

01:16:12.880 --> 01:16:14.616
I don't know how many
wasted years I've spent

01:16:14.640 --> 01:16:17.235
tracking down leads based
on female intuition.

01:16:17.320 --> 01:16:19.960
All right. I take it you didn't
find the trunk,

01:16:20.040 --> 01:16:23.511
and all this is from an old speech you
once made at the Policemen's Ball.

01:16:23.640 --> 01:16:26.109
I found the trunk
a half-hour after I left here.

01:16:26.200 --> 01:16:29.876
I suppose it's normal for a man
to tie up a trunk with heavy rope?

01:16:29.960 --> 01:16:31.952
Well, if the lock
is broken, yes.

01:16:32.040 --> 01:16:35.158
And what did you find inside the trunk?
A surly note to me?

01:16:35.240 --> 01:16:37.357
Mrs. Thorwald's clothes.

01:16:37.440 --> 01:16:40.831
Clean, well packed.
Not stylish, but presentable.

01:16:40.920 --> 01:16:42.752
Didn't you take them
off to the crime lab?

01:16:42.840 --> 01:16:45.719
I sent them on their
merry and legal way.

01:16:45.800 --> 01:16:48.599
Why, when a woman is taking
a simple, short trip,

01:16:48.680 --> 01:16:50.592
does she take everything
she owns?

01:16:50.680 --> 01:16:54.390
Let's let the female psychology
department handle that one.

01:16:55.360 --> 01:16:57.829
I would say it looked
as if she wasn't coming back.

01:16:57.920 --> 01:16:59.593
That's what's known
as a family problem.

01:16:59.680 --> 01:17:02.514
And if she wasn't coming back,
why didn't he tell his landlord?

01:17:02.600 --> 01:17:07.391
I'll tell you why he didn't tell his landlord.
Because he was hiding something.

01:17:09.520 --> 01:17:11.989
Do you tell your
landlord everything?

01:17:12.080 --> 01:17:13.799
I told you to
be careful, Tom.

01:17:13.880 --> 01:17:17.080
Look, if I'd have been careful piloting
that reconnaissance plane during the war,

01:17:17.120 --> 01:17:18.696
you wouldn't have had the chance
to take the pictures

01:17:18.720 --> 01:17:23.112
that won you a medal, and a good job,
and fame and money.

01:17:25.440 --> 01:17:30.037
What do you say we all sit down
and have a nice, friendly drink, too? Hmm?

01:17:30.800 --> 01:17:32.712
Forget all about this.

01:17:33.400 --> 01:17:36.996
We can tell lies about the good
old days during the war.

01:17:37.960 --> 01:17:40.031
You mean you're through
with the case?

01:17:40.120 --> 01:17:42.954
There is no case to be through with,
Miss Fremont.

01:17:43.040 --> 01:17:44.918
How about that drink?

01:17:52.520 --> 01:17:54.637
Yeah, I guess you're right.

01:17:55.880 --> 01:17:59.237
Well, I think I better get home
and get some sleep.

01:18:00.840 --> 01:18:02.274
Cheers.

01:18:06.720 --> 01:18:08.791
Not much of a snifter.

01:18:13.560 --> 01:18:16.200
Oh, Jeff, if you
need any more help...

01:18:16.280 --> 01:18:19.751
Consult the Yellow Pages
in your telephone directory.

01:18:20.440 --> 01:18:23.114
Oh, I love funny
exiting lines.

01:18:24.200 --> 01:18:25.873
Who was that trunk
addressed to?

01:18:25.960 --> 01:18:27.394
Mrs. Anna Thorwald.

01:18:27.480 --> 01:18:30.040
Well, then let's wait
and find out who picks it up.

01:18:30.120 --> 01:18:32.555
Oh, that telephone call.
I gave them your number.

01:18:32.640 --> 01:18:33.960
I hope you don't mind.

01:18:34.040 --> 01:18:35.554
Depends on who they were.

01:18:35.640 --> 01:18:37.199
The police at Merritsville.

01:18:37.280 --> 01:18:39.476
They reported the trunk was
just picked up

01:18:39.560 --> 01:18:41.517
by Mrs. Anna Thorwald.

01:18:42.440 --> 01:18:44.830
Don't stay up too late.

01:18:51.640 --> 01:18:53.313
(PEOPLE SINGING)

01:19:03.160 --> 01:19:05.072
- Look.
- What?

01:19:37.080 --> 01:19:39.640
JEFF: Yeah, he's kind
of young, isn't he?

01:20:15.520 --> 01:20:17.273
What are you doing?

01:20:18.080 --> 01:20:19.434
(SHOUTING)

01:20:22.600 --> 01:20:26.037
(BOTH ARGUING)

01:20:43.600 --> 01:20:48.197
You know, much as I hate to give
Thomas J. Doyle too much credit,

01:20:48.320 --> 01:20:50.596
he might have gotten a hold
of something when he said

01:20:50.680 --> 01:20:53.832
that was pretty private
stuff going on out there.

01:20:55.600 --> 01:21:00.755
I wonder if it's ethical to watch a man
with binoculars and a long-focus lens.

01:21:02.600 --> 01:21:05.513
Do you... Do you
suppose it's ethical,

01:21:05.600 --> 01:21:08.798
even if you prove that he didn't
commit a crime?

01:21:10.360 --> 01:21:13.034
I'm not much on
rear-window ethics.

01:21:13.120 --> 01:21:14.776
Of course, they can
do the same thing to me.

01:21:14.800 --> 01:21:18.271
Watch me like a bug under
a glass, if they want to.

01:21:18.360 --> 01:21:21.876
Jeff, you know, if someone came in here,
they wouldn't believe what they'd see.

01:21:21.960 --> 01:21:22.960
What?

01:21:23.040 --> 01:21:24.235
You and me with long faces.

01:21:24.320 --> 01:21:28.837
Plunged into despair because we find out
a man didn't kill his wife.

01:21:28.920 --> 01:21:32.391
We're two of the most frightening
ghouls I've ever known.

01:21:32.480 --> 01:21:37.430
You'd think we could be a little bit happy
that the poor woman is alive and well.

01:21:39.960 --> 01:21:43.317
Whatever happened to that old saying,
"Love thy neighbor"?

01:21:43.400 --> 01:21:44.800
(JEFF CHUCKLES)

01:21:45.000 --> 01:21:48.357
You know, I think I'll start reviving
that tomorrow.

01:21:49.560 --> 01:21:51.995
Yeah, I'll begin
with Miss Torso.

01:21:52.840 --> 01:21:56.720
Not if I have to move
into an apartment across the way

01:21:58.320 --> 01:22:01.518
and do the <i>Dance of the Seven Veils</i>
every hour.

01:22:04.480 --> 01:22:06.472
Show's over for tonight.

01:22:11.600 --> 01:22:13.910
Preview of coming
attractions.

01:22:16.360 --> 01:22:19.034
Did Mr. Doyle think
I stole this case?

01:22:20.520 --> 01:22:23.399
No, Lisa, I don't
think he did.

01:22:38.400 --> 01:22:40.312
What do you think?

01:22:40.400 --> 01:22:41.400
Well...

01:22:42.680 --> 01:22:44.751
I will rephrase the question.

01:22:44.840 --> 01:22:46.069
Thank you.

01:22:47.160 --> 01:22:48.753
Do you like it?

01:22:49.560 --> 01:22:50.914
Yes, I like it.

01:22:51.600 --> 01:22:53.956
- (WOMAN SCREAMING)
- Well...

01:22:54.040 --> 01:22:55.599
(WOMAN SOBBING)

01:23:02.600 --> 01:23:04.319
WOMAN 1: What's the matter?

01:23:04.400 --> 01:23:05.595
(CHATTERING)

01:23:07.080 --> 01:23:09.117
- MAN 1: Somebody's hurt?
- WOMAN 2: It's the dog.

01:23:09.200 --> 01:23:11.669
Something's happened
to the dog.

01:23:14.440 --> 01:23:17.672
MAN 2: What's the trouble?
Somebody fall out a window?

01:23:18.320 --> 01:23:20.630
MAN 3: See, it's a dog
down there.

01:23:21.600 --> 01:23:22.954
It's dead.

01:23:23.560 --> 01:23:26.359
It's been strangled.
The neck is broken.

01:23:27.200 --> 01:23:28.998
Which one of you did it?

01:23:29.080 --> 01:23:31.515
Which one of you
killed my dog?

01:23:33.200 --> 01:23:35.999
You don't know the meaning
of the word "Neighbors."

01:23:36.520 --> 01:23:40.036
Neighbors like each other,
speak to each other,

01:23:40.160 --> 01:23:42.470
care if anybody
lives or dies.

01:23:43.200 --> 01:23:44.953
But none of you do!

01:23:48.880 --> 01:23:51.349
But I couldn't imagine
any of you being so low

01:23:51.440 --> 01:23:54.831
that you'd kill a little
helpless, friendly dog.

01:23:57.000 --> 01:24:00.880
The only thing in this whole neighborhood
who liked anybody.

01:24:09.160 --> 01:24:11.914
Did you kill him
because he liked you?

01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:14.151
Just because he liked you?

01:24:16.960 --> 01:24:18.633
Let's go inside.

01:24:22.800 --> 01:24:25.713
Come on. Let's go back in.
It's only a dog.

01:24:38.080 --> 01:24:42.074
You know, for a minute, that Tom Doyle
almost had me convinced I was wrong.

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:44.072
- But you're not?
- Look.

01:24:44.160 --> 01:24:48.359
In the whole courtyard, only one person
didn't come to the window.

01:24:48.440 --> 01:24:49.669
Look.

01:24:55.040 --> 01:24:58.431
Why would Thorwald want to kill
a little dog?

01:25:00.320 --> 01:25:02.312
Because it knew too much?

01:25:10.360 --> 01:25:13.398
You think this is worth
waiting all day to see?

01:25:13.920 --> 01:25:15.434
LISA: Is he cleaning house?

01:25:15.520 --> 01:25:18.115
He's washing down
the bathroom walls.

01:25:18.200 --> 01:25:19.236
Hmm.

01:25:19.320 --> 01:25:21.437
Must have splattered a lot.

01:25:23.000 --> 01:25:25.720
Well, why not?
That's what we're all thinking.

01:25:25.800 --> 01:25:28.680
He killed her in there, he has to clean up
those stains before he leaves.

01:25:28.720 --> 01:25:31.360
Oh, Stella, your
choice of words.

01:25:31.480 --> 01:25:34.871
STELLA: Nobody ever invented
a polite word for a killing yet.

01:25:37.960 --> 01:25:42.955
Lisa? Lisa, back there, on that shelf,
there's a little yellow box. You see it?

01:25:43.040 --> 01:25:44.793
- Top one?
- Right on top, yeah.

01:25:44.880 --> 01:25:47.395
Bring it. And bring
me the viewer there.

01:25:49.280 --> 01:25:50.839
I just got a...

01:25:51.560 --> 01:25:54.314
Now, these are
about two weeks old.

01:25:54.400 --> 01:25:58.076
I hope I took something else besides
eg art. Now which one...

01:25:58.160 --> 01:26:00.516
- What are you looking for?
- Here.

01:26:01.000 --> 01:26:04.198
There's something, and if I'm right,
I think I've solved a murder.

01:26:04.280 --> 01:26:06.078
- Mrs. Thorwald?
- No.

01:26:07.240 --> 01:26:09.277
No, the dog.

01:26:10.440 --> 01:26:11.635
Uh-huh.

01:26:11.720 --> 01:26:14.713
I think I know
why Thorwald killed that dog.

01:26:15.240 --> 01:26:18.392
Here, now you take a look.
Tell me what you see.

01:26:18.720 --> 01:26:20.359
Now take it down.

01:26:20.840 --> 01:26:24.470
Now look again.
Now take it down. You see?

01:26:25.600 --> 01:26:28.160
But it's just a picture
of the backyard, that's all.

01:26:28.240 --> 01:26:32.234
One important change.
One important change.

01:26:33.080 --> 01:26:36.278
Those flowers in Thorwald's pet
flower bed.

01:26:37.200 --> 01:26:39.271
You mean where the dog
was sniffing around?

01:26:39.360 --> 01:26:42.831
Where the dog was digging.
Now look at those flowers.

01:26:43.240 --> 01:26:46.995
Look, right? Those two yellow
zinnias on this end

01:26:47.080 --> 01:26:48.912
aren't as tall as they were.

01:26:49.000 --> 01:26:52.311
Now, since when do flowers
grow shorter in two weeks?

01:26:52.400 --> 01:26:53.880
There's something
buried there.

01:26:53.960 --> 01:26:55.679
Mrs. Thorwald.

01:26:56.280 --> 01:26:59.557
You haven't spent much time
around cemeteries, have you?

01:26:59.640 --> 01:27:02.678
Mr. Thorwald could scarcely put
his wife's body

01:27:02.760 --> 01:27:05.719
in a plot of ground
about one foot square.

01:27:05.800 --> 01:27:08.634
Unless, of course, he put her
in standing on end,

01:27:08.720 --> 01:27:11.440
and then he wouldn't
need a knife and saw.

01:27:11.520 --> 01:27:15.196
No, my idea is she's
scattered all over town.

01:27:15.280 --> 01:27:17.556
- Leg in the East River...
- Oh, Stella, please!

01:27:17.640 --> 01:27:19.136
No, no, no. There's
something in there.

01:27:19.160 --> 01:27:21.720
Those flowers have been taken out
and put back in.

01:27:21.840 --> 01:27:23.672
- Maybe it's the knife and saw.
- Yeah.

01:27:23.800 --> 01:27:24.836
Call Lieutenant Doyle.

01:27:24.920 --> 01:27:26.070
No, let's wait.

01:27:26.160 --> 01:27:29.915
Let's wait till it gets a little darker,
and I'll go over there and dig them up.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:33.994
You'll go? You won't dig anything up
and get your neck broken.

01:27:34.640 --> 01:27:36.279
No, no, we've...

01:27:36.760 --> 01:27:40.117
We're not gonna call Doyle until
I can produce Mrs. Thorwald's body.

01:27:40.200 --> 01:27:43.034
What we've got to do is find
a way to get into that apartment.

01:27:43.120 --> 01:27:44.600
He's packing.

01:27:45.840 --> 01:27:46.956
Uh-oh.

01:27:47.040 --> 01:27:49.350
Here, get me a pencil.

01:27:50.280 --> 01:27:53.557
Stella, get me some note paper.
It's up here someplace.

01:27:53.640 --> 01:27:55.199
There it is.

01:29:11.840 --> 01:29:14.674
You did it, Thorwald.
You did it.

01:29:18.720 --> 01:29:19.800
Look out, Lisa. He's coming.

01:29:19.880 --> 01:29:21.997
You never should have let her do it.
If he ever...

01:29:22.080 --> 01:29:23.355
Look. Look. Look.

01:29:41.120 --> 01:29:42.349
(SIGHS)

01:29:43.280 --> 01:29:45.715
Well, thank heaven
that's over.

01:29:48.960 --> 01:29:49.976
All right if I have a drink?

01:29:50.000 --> 01:29:51.593
Sure, go ahead.

01:30:00.160 --> 01:30:04.074
There's no doubt about it. He's leaving.
It's just a question of when.

01:30:04.160 --> 01:30:06.800
Mind if I use that
portable keyhole?

01:30:08.120 --> 01:30:12.000
Go ahead, just as long as you tell me
what you're looking at.

01:30:18.000 --> 01:30:19.000
STELLA: I wonder...

01:30:19.080 --> 01:30:20.196
JEFF: Wonder what?

01:30:20.280 --> 01:30:22.158
Miss Lonelyheart's
just laid out something

01:30:22.240 --> 01:30:24.880
that looks like rhodium
tri-eckonal capsules.

01:30:24.960 --> 01:30:26.235
You can tell from here?

01:30:26.320 --> 01:30:29.279
I've handled enough of those red pills
to put everybody in Hackensack

01:30:29.400 --> 01:30:30.675
to sleep for the winter.

01:30:30.760 --> 01:30:32.433
Well, does she have
enough of them to...

01:30:32.520 --> 01:30:34.512
No. It will make
the rest easy to...

01:30:35.280 --> 01:30:36.999
Wasn't that close?

01:30:37.640 --> 01:30:40.758
What was his reaction. I mean,
when he looked at the note?

01:30:40.840 --> 01:30:44.754
Well, it wasn't the kind of an expression
that would get him a quick loan at the bank.

01:30:44.840 --> 01:30:46.672
Jeff, the handbag!

01:31:12.000 --> 01:31:14.356
Suppose Mrs. Thorwald's
wedding ring

01:31:14.440 --> 01:31:17.433
is among the jewelry
he has in that handbag?

01:31:17.520 --> 01:31:20.115
Now, during the phone conversation
he held up three rings.

01:31:20.200 --> 01:31:23.511
One with a diamond, one with
a big stone of some sort

01:31:23.600 --> 01:31:25.478
and then just
a plain gold band.

01:31:25.560 --> 01:31:29.634
And the last thing she would leave behind
would be a wedding ring.

01:31:29.720 --> 01:31:31.951
Stella, do you ever
leave yours at home?

01:31:32.040 --> 01:31:36.478
The only way anybody could get that ring
would be to chop off my finger.

01:31:39.480 --> 01:31:42.678
Let's go down and find out
what's buried in the garden.

01:31:42.760 --> 01:31:47.118
Why not? I've always wanted
to meet Mrs. Thorwald.

01:31:47.240 --> 01:31:48.896
- What are you two talking about?
- You got a shovel?

01:31:48.920 --> 01:31:50.216
A shovel? Of course
I don't have a shovel.

01:31:50.240 --> 01:31:52.038
There's probably
one in the basement.

01:31:52.120 --> 01:31:54.157
Jeff, if you're squeamish,
just don't look.

01:31:54.240 --> 01:31:55.560
Squeamish? I'm not squeamish.

01:31:55.640 --> 01:31:59.190
I just don't want you two to end up
like that dog ended up. That's all.

01:31:59.280 --> 01:32:02.512
Oh, you know, Miss Fremont,
he might just have something there.

01:32:02.600 --> 01:32:04.671
Just hold on.
Here, take this.

01:32:04.760 --> 01:32:07.116
No sense taking any
chances in this thing.

01:32:07.200 --> 01:32:09.431
Here, give me the phone book.

01:32:10.600 --> 01:32:11.875
What for?

01:32:12.600 --> 01:32:14.557
Maybe I can get him
out of that apartment.

01:32:14.640 --> 01:32:16.154
We only need a few minutes.

01:32:16.240 --> 01:32:17.959
I'll see if I can
get about 15 minutes.

01:32:18.040 --> 01:32:19.076
How?

01:32:19.160 --> 01:32:20.160
Thorwald.

01:32:21.800 --> 01:32:24.634
Thorwald... Thorwald...

01:32:26.080 --> 01:32:29.391
Chelsea 27099.

01:32:30.320 --> 01:32:31.879
27099.

01:32:31.960 --> 01:32:35.112
We scared him once,
maybe we can scare him again.

01:32:35.880 --> 01:32:38.873
Well, I guess I'm using that word
"we" a little freely.

01:32:38.960 --> 01:32:40.917
I mean, you're taking
all the chances.

01:32:41.000 --> 01:32:42.912
LISA: Shall we vote
him in, Stella?

01:32:43.000 --> 01:32:44.036
STELLA: Unanimously.

01:32:44.160 --> 01:32:46.436
27099. Look out.

01:32:49.720 --> 01:32:51.040
Chelsea.

01:32:53.080 --> 01:32:54.400
Chelsea.

01:32:59.280 --> 01:33:00.839
(PHONE RINGING)

01:33:12.360 --> 01:33:14.591
Go on, pick it up, Thorwald.

01:33:15.600 --> 01:33:17.478
Go on, you're curious.

01:33:18.360 --> 01:33:21.592
You wonder if it's your girlfriend calling,
the one you killed for.

01:33:21.680 --> 01:33:23.353
Go on, pick it up.

01:33:27.640 --> 01:33:28.790
<i>THORWALD: Hello?</i>

01:33:29.760 --> 01:33:31.638
Did you get my note?

01:33:33.680 --> 01:33:35.911
Well, did you get it,
Thorwald?

01:33:36.000 --> 01:33:37.150
<i>Who are you?</i>

01:33:37.240 --> 01:33:39.436
I'll give you a chance
to find out.

01:33:39.520 --> 01:33:42.274
Meet me in the bar
at the Albert Hotel.

01:33:42.360 --> 01:33:44.113
Do it right away.

01:33:44.200 --> 01:33:45.793
<i>Why should I?</i>

01:33:45.880 --> 01:33:47.394
A little business meeting...

01:33:48.160 --> 01:33:50.880
To settle the estate
of your late wife.

01:33:51.280 --> 01:33:54.910
<i>I don't know what you mean.</i>

01:33:55.000 --> 01:33:59.438
Come on. Quit stalling, Thorwald,
or I'll hang up and call the police.

01:33:59.760 --> 01:34:01.672
<i>I have only $100 or so.</i>

01:34:01.760 --> 01:34:03.035
That's a start.

01:34:03.560 --> 01:34:07.236
I'm at the Albert now.
I'll be looking for you.

01:34:26.720 --> 01:34:28.313
Come on, Stella, let's go.

01:34:28.400 --> 01:34:29.993
One of you keep an eye
on this window.

01:34:30.080 --> 01:34:33.676
If I see him coming back,
I'll signal with a flashbulb.

01:36:08.160 --> 01:36:09.833
<i>WOMAN: This is
the Doyle residence.</i>

01:36:09.920 --> 01:36:14.153
Hello, this is L.B. Jefferies speaking.
I'm a friend of Mr. Doyle.

01:36:14.440 --> 01:36:15.440
Who's this?

01:36:15.520 --> 01:36:16.636
<i>This is a babysitter.</i>

01:36:16.720 --> 01:36:18.200
Oh. Uh...

01:36:18.280 --> 01:36:19.509
When do you expect them in?

01:36:19.600 --> 01:36:24.152
<i>Well, I'm hired till 1:00. They went
to dinner and maybe a nightclub.</i>

01:36:24.520 --> 01:36:25.874
I see.

01:36:26.360 --> 01:36:29.990
If he calls in, would you have him get
in touch with L.B. Jefferies right away?

01:36:30.080 --> 01:36:31.992
I might have quite
a surprise for him.

01:36:32.080 --> 01:36:34.993
<i>Well, do we have your number,
Mr. Jefferies?</i>

01:36:35.080 --> 01:36:37.197
He has it. Good night.

01:36:37.280 --> 01:36:38.760
<i>Good night.</i>

01:36:56.720 --> 01:36:59.519
JEFF: Stella was wrong
about Miss Lonelyheart.

01:37:26.360 --> 01:37:27.555
(INAUDIBLE)

01:37:31.400 --> 01:37:33.790
Lisa, what are you...
Don't do...

01:37:49.960 --> 01:37:52.600
Lisa, what are you
doing? Don't go...

01:38:26.440 --> 01:38:29.080
Come on, come on!
Get out of there!

01:38:35.320 --> 01:38:39.200
She said ring Thorwald's phone
the second you see him come back.

01:38:39.280 --> 01:38:40.296
I'm gonna ring it right now!

01:38:40.320 --> 01:38:43.074
Oh, no. Give her
another minute.

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:48.915
MAN: All right, fellows, let's try it
once from the beginning.

01:38:49.000 --> 01:38:50.753
Miss Lonelyheart.

01:38:50.840 --> 01:38:52.718
Oh, call the police!

01:38:55.080 --> 01:38:56.080
<i>WOMAN: Operator.</i>

01:38:56.200 --> 01:38:58.271
Get me the police,
sixth precinct.

01:38:58.360 --> 01:38:59.680
<i>Yes, sir.</i>

01:39:11.880 --> 01:39:14.554
STELLA: Mr. Jefferies,
the music stopped her.

01:39:22.080 --> 01:39:23.799
What... Lisa!

01:39:26.200 --> 01:39:27.350
Lisa!

01:39:38.640 --> 01:39:40.359
<i>Precinct 6, Sergeant Allgood.</i>

01:39:40.440 --> 01:39:45.595
Hello. Hello. Look, a man is assaulting
a woman at 125 West 9th Street.

01:39:45.680 --> 01:39:49.230
Second floor, at the rear.
Make it fast!

01:39:49.320 --> 01:39:50.320
<i>Your name?</i>

01:39:50.400 --> 01:39:52.039
L.B. Jefferies.

01:39:52.120 --> 01:39:53.120
<i>Phone number?</i>

01:39:53.200 --> 01:39:55.476
Chelsea 25598.

01:40:12.040 --> 01:40:13.599
(INAUDIBLE)

01:40:16.080 --> 01:40:17.753
The door was open.

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:31.957
(CHATTERING)

01:40:39.800 --> 01:40:41.837
- I told you...
- Oh, no!

01:40:43.720 --> 01:40:45.712
Let go of me! Jeff!

01:40:45.800 --> 01:40:46.995
Oh, no!

01:40:47.080 --> 01:40:48.639
LISA: Jeff! Jeff!

01:40:49.120 --> 01:40:50.190
Jeff!

01:40:51.760 --> 01:40:52.955
Lisa!

01:40:53.960 --> 01:40:56.555
Stella, what do we do?

01:40:56.640 --> 01:40:57.835
Jeff!

01:40:58.640 --> 01:41:00.871
- Jeff!
- STELLA: Well, here they come.

01:41:03.000 --> 01:41:04.229
(SIGHS)

01:41:29.080 --> 01:41:30.673
(INAUDIBLE)

01:41:50.920 --> 01:41:53.216
STELLA: What's she trying to do?
Why doesn't she turn him in?

01:41:53.240 --> 01:41:54.560
She's a smart girl.

01:41:54.640 --> 01:41:56.280
"Smart girl"?
She'll get herself arrested.

01:41:56.360 --> 01:41:59.034
It'll get her out
of there, won't it?

01:42:01.680 --> 01:42:03.592
Look, the wedding ring!

01:42:09.400 --> 01:42:11.869
Turn off the light!
He's seen us!

01:42:21.800 --> 01:42:23.473
How long do you think
he'll stay there?

01:42:23.560 --> 01:42:27.634
Well, unless he's dumber than I think he is,
he won't wait until his lease is up.

01:42:27.720 --> 01:42:29.776
Get my billfold out of the drawer
in the table there.

01:42:29.800 --> 01:42:31.029
What do you need money for?

01:42:31.120 --> 01:42:32.679
I'm gonna bail
Lisa out of jail.

01:42:32.760 --> 01:42:35.116
You know, you could leave her
there till next Tuesday,

01:42:35.200 --> 01:42:37.635
then you could sneak
safely away as planned.

01:42:37.720 --> 01:42:41.634
Yeah, let's see. $127.

01:42:41.720 --> 01:42:42.790
How much do you need?

01:42:42.880 --> 01:42:46.271
Well, this is first-offense burglary,
that's about $250.

01:42:46.360 --> 01:42:48.238
- Lisa's handbag.
- Yeah.

01:42:49.600 --> 01:42:50.670
How much does she have?

01:42:50.760 --> 01:42:52.035
Fifty cents.

01:42:52.120 --> 01:42:53.315
Here, take this.

01:42:53.400 --> 01:42:55.312
Look, I got $20 or so
in my purse.

01:42:55.400 --> 01:42:56.896
What about the rest?
What about the rest?

01:42:56.920 --> 01:42:59.480
When those cops see Lisa,
they'll even contribute.

01:42:59.560 --> 01:43:00.835
Well, hurry up.

01:43:00.920 --> 01:43:02.957
(PHONE RINGING)

01:43:05.080 --> 01:43:07.436
Just a minute. Hurry up!

01:43:08.080 --> 01:43:09.116
Jefferies.

01:43:09.200 --> 01:43:10.839
<i>DOYLE: All right.
What is it now?</i>

01:43:10.920 --> 01:43:14.118
Doyle, I've got
something really big for you.

01:43:14.200 --> 01:43:16.112
<i>Why did I have to
return your call?</i>

01:43:16.200 --> 01:43:19.398
<i>Look, Jeff! Don't louse up my night
with another mad killer...</i>

01:43:19.480 --> 01:43:24.077
Listen to me! Listen to me!
Lisa's in jail. She got arrested.

01:43:24.440 --> 01:43:26.159
<i>- Your Lisa?</i>
- My Lisa.

01:43:26.240 --> 01:43:30.075
Boy, you should have seen her.
She got into Thorwald's apartment.

01:43:30.160 --> 01:43:33.376
But then he came back, and the only way
I could get her out was to call the police.

01:43:33.400 --> 01:43:35.039
<i>- I told you...</i>
- I know you told me!

01:43:35.120 --> 01:43:38.192
She went in to get evidence
and she came out with evidence.

01:43:38.280 --> 01:43:39.919
<i>- Like what?</i>
- Like what?

01:43:40.000 --> 01:43:42.913
Like Mrs. Thorwald's wedding ring.
That's like what.

01:43:43.000 --> 01:43:46.789
If that woman was alive,
she'd be wearing that ring. Right?

01:43:47.960 --> 01:43:49.997
<i>- A possibility.</i>
- A possibility. It's a fact!

01:43:50.080 --> 01:43:54.120
He killed a dog last night because the dog was
scratching around in the garden.

01:43:54.200 --> 01:43:58.353
You know why? Because he had something buried
in that garden that the dog scented.

01:43:58.440 --> 01:43:59.556
<i>Like an old ham bone?</i>

01:43:59.640 --> 01:44:02.712
Look, I don't know what pet names
Thorwald had for his wife,

01:44:02.840 --> 01:44:04.194
but I'll tell you this,

01:44:04.280 --> 01:44:07.512
all those trips at night
with that metal suitcase,

01:44:07.600 --> 01:44:09.239
he wasn't taking
out his possessions,

01:44:09.320 --> 01:44:12.757
because his possessions
are still up in the apartment.

01:44:12.840 --> 01:44:14.479
<i>Perhaps it was
old "ham bone."</i>

01:44:14.600 --> 01:44:16.193
Yeah, in sections.

01:44:16.280 --> 01:44:17.634
And I'll tell you
something else.

01:44:17.720 --> 01:44:20.792
All the telephone calls he made were
long distance, all right?

01:44:20.880 --> 01:44:24.635
Now, if he called his wife
long distance on the day she left,

01:44:24.720 --> 01:44:26.916
after she arrived
in Merritsville,

01:44:27.000 --> 01:44:30.277
why did she write a card to him saying that
she'd arrived in Merritsville?

01:44:30.360 --> 01:44:32.192
Why did she do that?

01:44:32.920 --> 01:44:34.195
<i>Where'd they take Lisa?</i>

01:44:34.280 --> 01:44:35.280
Precinct 6.

01:44:35.360 --> 01:44:36.736
I sent somebody over
with the bail money.

01:44:36.760 --> 01:44:38.194
<i>Maybe you won't need it.</i>

01:44:38.280 --> 01:44:39.280
<i>I'll run it down, Jeff.</i>

01:44:39.360 --> 01:44:41.431
All right. All right.
Now hurry up, will you?

01:44:41.520 --> 01:44:43.352
This fellow knows
he's being watched.

01:44:43.440 --> 01:44:45.272
He's not gonna wait
around forever. Hurry up.

01:44:45.360 --> 01:44:47.352
<i>If that ring checks out,
we'll give him an escort.</i>

01:44:47.440 --> 01:44:49.557
<i>- So long, Jeff.</i>
- So long.

01:44:56.320 --> 01:44:57.640
(PHONE RINGING)

01:44:57.720 --> 01:45:01.555
Hello. Hello. Tom, I think
Thorwald's left. I don't see...

01:45:04.240 --> 01:45:05.640
Hello...

01:45:33.040 --> 01:45:34.554
(DOOR SLAMMING)

01:46:01.160 --> 01:46:03.117
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

01:47:37.440 --> 01:47:39.432
What do you want from me?

01:47:43.360 --> 01:47:45.431
Your friend, the girl,
could have turned me in.

01:47:45.560 --> 01:47:47.074
Why didn't she?

01:47:49.200 --> 01:47:52.989
What is it you want? A lot of money?
I don't have any money.

01:47:56.720 --> 01:47:58.313
Say something.

01:48:00.040 --> 01:48:02.953
Say something!
Tell me what you want!

01:48:06.120 --> 01:48:08.351
Can you get me
that ring back?

01:48:09.520 --> 01:48:11.000
No.

01:48:11.080 --> 01:48:12.912
Tell her to bring it back!

01:48:13.000 --> 01:48:15.117
I can't. The police
have it by now.

01:48:52.280 --> 01:48:54.112
Lisa! Doyle!

01:49:02.520 --> 01:49:03.954
WOMAN: Over there!

01:49:09.800 --> 01:49:12.269
Look! Look, over
in that apartment!

01:49:31.320 --> 01:49:32.640
JEFF: Doyle!

01:49:36.720 --> 01:49:38.837
Creele! Give me your .38!

01:49:44.280 --> 01:49:45.953
(WOMAN SCREAMING)

01:49:49.840 --> 01:49:51.656
I'm sorry, Jeff.
I got here as fast as I could!

01:49:51.680 --> 01:49:53.496
Oh, Mr. Jefferies!
Don't let anybody touch him.

01:49:53.520 --> 01:49:55.398
Get my medical bag
from upstairs.

01:49:55.480 --> 01:49:57.597
Oh, Lisa, sweetie,
if anything had happened to you...

01:49:57.680 --> 01:49:59.433
Oh, shut up. I'm all right.

01:49:59.520 --> 01:50:01.477
Gee, I'm proud of you!

01:50:02.520 --> 01:50:04.876
You got enough for
a search warrant now?

01:50:04.960 --> 01:50:06.155
Oh, yeah, sure.

01:50:06.240 --> 01:50:08.277
- DETECTIVE: Lieutenant Doyle!
- Yeah?

01:50:08.360 --> 01:50:09.430
Is he okay?

01:50:09.520 --> 01:50:10.556
He's alive.

01:50:10.640 --> 01:50:14.190
Thorwald's ready to take us
on a tour of the East River.

01:50:19.520 --> 01:50:21.671
Did he say what was
buried in the flower bed?

01:50:21.800 --> 01:50:25.919
Yeah. He said the dog got
too inquisitive, so he dug it up.

01:50:26.520 --> 01:50:29.354
It's in a hat box over
in his apartment.

01:50:29.880 --> 01:50:31.000
- Want to look?
- No, thanks.

01:50:31.080 --> 01:50:33.276
I don't want any part of her.

01:50:39.520 --> 01:50:42.319
I hope it's gonna be a hit.
This is the first release.

01:50:42.400 --> 01:50:44.312
I'd love to hear it.

01:50:44.400 --> 01:50:47.711
I can't tell you what this music
has meant to me.

01:50:48.280 --> 01:50:49.839
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

01:50:57.560 --> 01:51:03.079
<i>MAN: (SINGING)
Lisa, with your starry eyes</i>

01:51:03.160 --> 01:51:05.197
Now, let's try
it again, puppy.

01:51:05.280 --> 01:51:07.476
Stay still. Stay.

01:51:07.560 --> 01:51:13.670
<i>Hold me and whisper the sweet words
I'm yearning for</i>

01:51:13.760 --> 01:51:15.194
<i>Drown me in kisses</i>

01:51:15.280 --> 01:51:16.760
- Stanley!
- Hi, baby!

01:51:17.000 --> 01:51:19.754
<i>Caresses I'm burning for</i>

01:51:19.840 --> 01:51:22.639
My, look what the Army's done
for you!

01:51:22.720 --> 01:51:24.473
<i>Lisa, every touch is
new ecstasy</i>

01:51:24.560 --> 01:51:28.600
The Army's made me hungry.
What've you got in the icebox to eat?

01:51:29.280 --> 01:51:31.351
Boy, it's good to be home.

01:51:31.520 --> 01:51:34.672
<i>Lisa, angels dance
when you cling to me</i>

01:51:34.760 --> 01:51:36.353
I don't care
if you help me or not,

01:51:36.440 --> 01:51:39.433
but if you'd told me you'd quit your job,
we wouldn't have gotten married.

01:51:39.520 --> 01:51:41.398
Oh, honey, come on...

01:51:41.480 --> 01:51:46.430
<i>If this is dreaming</i>

01:51:46.560 --> 01:51:52.431
<i>I hope I'll never wake</i>

01:51:54.280 --> 01:52:00.197
<i>But dream forever</i>

01:52:01.480 --> 01:52:08.080
<i>In your arms, oh, Lisa</i>

01:52:11.200 --> 01:52:15.160
<i>Lisa</i>
